WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.942 align:middle line:90%


00:00:10.942 --> 00:00:12.400 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
So we have already

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:16.353 align:middle line:84%
talked for a while about
various kinds of ambiguity.

00:00:16.353 --> 00:00:18.520 align:middle line:84%
And today, we're going to
talk about ambiguity more.

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:20.978 align:middle line:84%
This is, if anything, the best
time to talk about ambiguity

00:00:20.978 --> 00:00:23.497 align:middle line:84%
because we're now
actually into semantics.

00:00:23.497 --> 00:00:25.330 align:middle line:84%
The kinds of ambiguity
we were talking about

00:00:25.330 --> 00:00:29.350 align:middle line:84%
before were kinds
of what's sometimes

00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:31.810 align:middle line:84%
called structural
ambiguity-- that is,

00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:34.630 align:middle line:84%
sentences that had
more than one meaning.

00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:37.895 align:middle line:84%
The way we were talking about
it was had more than one

00:00:37.895 --> 00:00:39.520 align:middle line:84%
meaning because it
was possible to draw

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:41.240 align:middle line:90%
more than one tree for them.

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:43.870 align:middle line:84%
So the sentence that we spend
a lot of time talking about

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:46.120 align:middle line:84%
was I once shot an
elephant in my pajamas,

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:52.390 align:middle line:84%
where the whole joke there
was that "in my pajamas" could

00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:54.628 align:middle line:84%
be attached in a couple
of different places.

00:00:54.628 --> 00:00:56.170 align:middle line:84%
It could either
modify "the elephant"

00:00:56.170 --> 00:00:58.540 align:middle line:84%
or it could modify the verb
phrase "shot an elephant."

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:02.290 align:middle line:84%
And the upshot of that
was, well, the pajamas

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:04.209 align:middle line:84%
could be on either
of you, and yeah--

00:01:04.209 --> 00:01:04.959 align:middle line:90%
ambiguity.

00:01:04.959 --> 00:01:08.278 align:middle line:84%
Similar kinds of ambiguities
in these other examples.

00:01:08.278 --> 00:01:09.820 align:middle line:84%
But there's another
kind of ambiguity

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:11.890 align:middle line:84%
that's the one I want
to talk about today.

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:14.650 align:middle line:90%


00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:17.830 align:middle line:84%
People have claimed that
this sentence is ambiguous--

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:19.690 align:middle line:90%
"someone loves everyone."

00:01:19.690 --> 00:01:22.100 align:middle line:84%
The claim is this can
mean at least two things.

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:25.930 align:middle line:84%
It can mean-- let's get some
lights on up here in the front.

00:01:25.930 --> 00:01:28.750 align:middle line:90%


00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:31.450 align:middle line:90%
Oh dear, so many options.

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:36.290 align:middle line:90%


00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:38.510 align:middle line:90%
Stage left, stage right--

00:01:38.510 --> 00:01:40.480 align:middle line:90%
sure.

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:41.230 align:middle line:90%
Oh, I see.

00:01:41.230 --> 00:01:44.110 align:middle line:90%


00:01:44.110 --> 00:01:44.950 align:middle line:90%
That's what I want.

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:49.000 align:middle line:90%


00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:51.520 align:middle line:84%
Two kinds of meanings for this
that people have posited--

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:59.620 align:middle line:84%
one meaning in which it means
the following situation holds

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:01.390 align:middle line:90%
everyone is loved.

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:07.390 align:middle line:84%
That is, for each person, there
is someone that loves them.

00:02:07.390 --> 00:02:11.380 align:middle line:84%
That's what's diagrammed
in that first diagram

00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:16.375 align:middle line:84%
up there, where the love
relation holds between X and A

00:02:16.375 --> 00:02:16.900 align:middle line:90%
and B--

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:19.840 align:middle line:84%
X loves both A and B,
and between Y and C,

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:23.020 align:middle line:84%
and between Z and both
D and E. So that's

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:24.972 align:middle line:84%
a possible reading
for the sentence.

00:02:24.972 --> 00:02:26.680 align:middle line:84%
And then there's
another possible reading

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:29.380 align:middle line:84%
for the sentence,
and then there are

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:33.160 align:middle line:84%
arguments about whether this
is a different reading or not,

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:35.320 align:middle line:84%
in which it means
someone loves everyone

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:38.350 align:middle line:84%
what that means is there is
a person who loves everyone.

00:02:38.350 --> 00:02:39.760 align:middle line:90%
Maybe it's my grandma--

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:40.880 align:middle line:90%
very loving person.

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:43.700 align:middle line:90%
She loves absolutely everyone.

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:46.300 align:middle line:84%
And so the love relation
holds between X and absolutely

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:49.010 align:middle line:90%
everyone, including herself.

00:02:49.010 --> 00:02:51.890 align:middle line:84%
So that's a kind of ambiguity
people have claimed to exist.

00:02:51.890 --> 00:02:54.730 align:middle line:84%
I don't want us to get too
hung up on whether it does.

00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:57.640 align:middle line:84%
There are clearer
examples maybe.

00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:00.400 align:middle line:84%
Think about a sentence
like "Everyone in this room

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:01.810 align:middle line:90%
speaks two languages."

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:04.215 align:middle line:90%


00:03:04.215 --> 00:03:06.340 align:middle line:84%
This is going to be a
clearer example because we'll

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:09.550 align:middle line:84%
see that the availability
of the two meanings

00:03:09.550 --> 00:03:12.160 align:middle line:84%
can be affected by
things that we can do.

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:14.900 align:middle line:90%
What's something this can mean?

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:16.200 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:18.270 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: For every
individual, it's

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:21.150 align:middle line:84%
true that that individual
speaks two different languages.

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:22.775 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Yeah, so it's possible

00:03:22.775 --> 00:03:25.380 align:middle line:84%
that it means everyone in
this room is bilingual.

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:28.420 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I speak
English and Spanish,

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:31.530 align:middle line:84%
and you speak
Ukrainian and Polish,

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:34.170 align:middle line:84%
and you speak
Mandarin and Japanese.

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:35.930 align:middle line:84%
So everybody in this
room is bilingual.

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:38.700 align:middle line:90%
It could mean that.

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:40.470 align:middle line:84%
What's another
thing it can mean?

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:41.092 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:03:41.092 --> 00:03:43.050 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: If you put
together all the languages

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:45.300 align:middle line:84%
that everyone in this room
knew, it would only be two.

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:46.633 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Right, exactly.

00:03:46.633 --> 00:03:48.780 align:middle line:84%
So if you took a survey
of everybody in this room

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:50.780 align:middle line:84%
and found out all the
languages that they speak,

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:52.980 align:middle line:84%
there would be two things,
two languages that we all

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:53.920 align:middle line:90%
have in common.

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:57.360 align:middle line:84%
Maybe we all speak English
and we all speak Tagalog.

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:00.060 align:middle line:84%
Maybe some of you speak 12
languages, but two of them

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:01.590 align:middle line:90%
are English and Tagalog.

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:03.260 align:middle line:84%
That's another thing
that it could mean.

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:03.760 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:09.030 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I don't actually read
the sentence that second way.

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:11.700 align:middle line:84%
If everyone in this room
speaks two languages,

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:15.190 align:middle line:84%
as in collectively, everyone in
this room speaks two languages.

00:04:15.190 --> 00:04:16.720 align:middle line:90%
That just doesn't--

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:17.980 align:middle line:90%
I don't read it like that.

00:04:17.980 --> 00:04:20.522 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: That's not the
first reading you get for it.

00:04:20.522 --> 00:04:21.920 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, I agree with
you, actually.

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:24.250 align:middle line:84%
So what you're
claiming is that if I

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:26.200 align:middle line:84%
were to say "Everyone
in this room

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:28.948 align:middle line:84%
speaks two languages,
namely English and Tagalog,"

00:04:28.948 --> 00:04:31.240 align:middle line:84%
your interpretation would be
that everyone in this room

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:32.740 align:middle line:84%
is bilingual in
English and Tagalog.

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:35.957 align:middle line:84%
(Tagalog is a language
from the Philippines.)

00:04:35.957 --> 00:04:37.540 align:middle line:84%
It's a lot easier
to get that reading,

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:39.790 align:middle line:84%
I agree with you, than it
is to get the other reading,

00:04:39.790 --> 00:04:42.340 align:middle line:84%
where it just means everyone
in this room is bilingual.

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:42.970 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:04:42.970 --> 00:04:46.960 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think I
would have [INAUDIBLE]

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:50.357 align:middle line:90%
something to me in total.

00:04:50.357 --> 00:04:51.940 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Everyone in this room

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:54.460 align:middle line:90%
speaks two languages between us.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:56.918 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, you feel as though
there could be modifications

00:04:56.918 --> 00:04:58.960 align:middle line:84%
that you could make that
would make that reading.

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:00.550 align:middle line:84%
Here's one modification
you can make that makes

00:05:00.550 --> 00:05:01.467 align:middle line:90%
that reading come out.

00:05:01.467 --> 00:05:03.160 align:middle line:90%
You can passivize the sentence.

00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:08.410 align:middle line:84%
So "Two languages are spoken
by everyone in this room,"

00:05:08.410 --> 00:05:10.690 align:middle line:84%
I think has that
reading really clear.

00:05:10.690 --> 00:05:13.750 align:middle line:84%
There are two languages
that we all have in common.

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:16.098 align:middle line:84%
It's possible that it can
also mean the other thing,

00:05:16.098 --> 00:05:17.890 align:middle line:84%
that it can also mean
everyone in this room

00:05:17.890 --> 00:05:21.880 align:middle line:84%
is bilingual, but at
least can mean that.

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:26.140 align:middle line:84%
The fact that we can have
this sensation that these two

00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:29.860 align:middle line:84%
imaginable readings are
maybe both available for both

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:32.350 align:middle line:84%
of these sentences, maybe
not, but certainly one

00:05:32.350 --> 00:05:35.530 align:middle line:84%
is more available than
the other depending

00:05:35.530 --> 00:05:37.697 align:middle line:84%
on whether the sentence
is active or passive--

00:05:37.697 --> 00:05:39.280 align:middle line:84%
it suggests we want
to be able to talk

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:41.830 align:middle line:84%
about this kind of
difference in meaning

00:05:41.830 --> 00:05:42.970 align:middle line:90%
between these sentences.

00:05:42.970 --> 00:05:45.430 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to develop
the tools to do that today.

00:05:45.430 --> 00:05:50.030 align:middle line:90%


00:05:50.030 --> 00:05:52.250 align:middle line:84%
So in order to do
that, we're going

00:05:52.250 --> 00:05:54.980 align:middle line:84%
to need to develop a theory of
a certain special kind of noun

00:05:54.980 --> 00:05:57.320 align:middle line:84%
phrase-- what's
called a quantifier.

00:05:57.320 --> 00:05:58.820 align:middle line:84%
And to do that,
we're going to start

00:05:58.820 --> 00:06:01.230 align:middle line:84%
by talking about what noun
phrases mean more generally.

00:06:01.230 --> 00:06:03.140 align:middle line:90%
So here's a noun phrase--

00:06:03.140 --> 00:06:07.730 align:middle line:84%
refers to a person who's
the TA for some of you.

00:06:07.730 --> 00:06:11.643 align:middle line:84%
And if you were to ask, what
does that noun phrase mean?

00:06:11.643 --> 00:06:13.310 align:middle line:84%
Well, we talked about
this a little bit.

00:06:13.310 --> 00:06:16.550 align:middle line:84%
I had the example of there
are various things that you

00:06:16.550 --> 00:06:18.785 align:middle line:90%
could use to refer to me.

00:06:18.785 --> 00:06:20.660 align:middle line:84%
You could refer to me
as "Professor Richards"

00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:23.630 align:middle line:84%
or as "Norvin" or "that
so-and-so who gave me a C"--

00:06:23.630 --> 00:06:25.500 align:middle line:90%
and we're talking about that.

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:29.270 align:middle line:84%
So similarly, here's a phrase
that if you ask what it means,

00:06:29.270 --> 00:06:32.570 align:middle line:84%
you might expect that if we look
this up in your mental lexicon,

00:06:32.570 --> 00:06:35.810 align:middle line:84%
what we would find is
a picture of this guy--

00:06:35.810 --> 00:06:38.390 align:middle line:84%
those of you who have
him as your TA, at least.

00:06:38.390 --> 00:06:39.890 align:middle line:84%
So if you don't
have him as your TA,

00:06:39.890 --> 00:06:42.223 align:middle line:84%
maybe you don't know who he
is, but he's one of the TAs.

00:06:42.223 --> 00:06:44.450 align:middle line:90%


00:06:44.450 --> 00:06:48.560 align:middle line:84%
And to say, for example, "Enrico
Flor is an avid hangglider"

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:51.030 align:middle line:90%
is to say something like--

00:06:51.030 --> 00:06:54.290 align:middle line:84%
we ask what that means, it's
going to mean something like--

00:06:54.290 --> 00:06:57.092 align:middle line:84%
we'll have to figure out the
meaning of avid hangglider.

00:06:57.092 --> 00:06:59.300 align:middle line:84%
What is it to say that
someone is an avid hangglider?

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:01.160 align:middle line:84%
But when we figure
out what that is,

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:02.780 align:middle line:84%
we'll have a list
of all of the people

00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:04.070 align:middle line:90%
who are avid hanggliders.

00:07:04.070 --> 00:07:08.360 align:middle line:84%
And this claim is that list will
have Enrico Flor's name on it.

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:11.310 align:middle line:90%
That's what that means.

00:07:11.310 --> 00:07:15.350 align:middle line:84%
So that's a comparatively simple
meaning for a noun phrase.

00:07:15.350 --> 00:07:18.350 align:middle line:84%
Enrico Flor-- that noun
phrase refers to that guy.

00:07:18.350 --> 00:07:20.960 align:middle line:90%


00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:24.380 align:middle line:84%
What if I wanted to tell
you, the 24.900 TAs are avid

00:07:24.380 --> 00:07:25.760 align:middle line:90%
hanggliders.

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:29.150 align:middle line:84%
So again, we're going to have
a list of the avid hanggliders.

00:07:29.150 --> 00:07:32.780 align:middle line:90%
What does this sentence say?

00:07:32.780 --> 00:07:34.640 align:middle line:90%
What does it mean?

00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:38.270 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: You first have to
parse what the 24.900 TAs are

00:07:38.270 --> 00:07:42.260 align:middle line:84%
and decompose that into a list
of people and match them up

00:07:42.260 --> 00:07:44.898 align:middle line:90%
with who are the [INAUDIBLE].

00:07:44.898 --> 00:07:46.190 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah, exactly.

00:07:46.190 --> 00:07:47.450 align:middle line:90%
That's a nice way to say it.

00:07:47.450 --> 00:07:49.700 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to have a
list of the avid hanggliders,

00:07:49.700 --> 00:07:52.057 align:middle line:84%
and we're going to have
a list of the 24.900 TAs.

00:07:52.057 --> 00:07:54.140 align:middle line:84%
It'll have Enrico Flor,
and it'll have Yash Sinha,

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:58.010 align:middle line:84%
and it'll have all
four of the TAs.

00:07:58.010 --> 00:07:59.875 align:middle line:84%
And what this says
is if you look

00:07:59.875 --> 00:08:01.250 align:middle line:84%
at that list of
avid hanggliders,

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:02.958 align:middle line:84%
it'll have all four
of those names on it.

00:08:02.958 --> 00:08:07.320 align:middle line:90%


00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:09.960 align:middle line:84%
I'm trying to lure you into a
false sense of security here.

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:13.250 align:middle line:84%
So far, this should
seem pretty simple.

00:08:13.250 --> 00:08:14.890 align:middle line:90%
So yeah, there's a list--

00:08:14.890 --> 00:08:15.430 align:middle line:90%
set.

00:08:15.430 --> 00:08:16.630 align:middle line:90%
I've made it a set here.

00:08:16.630 --> 00:08:20.380 align:middle line:84%
It's a set that contains Enrico
and Peter and Yash and Anton.

00:08:20.380 --> 00:08:23.890 align:middle line:84%
And what we're
saying is that set

00:08:23.890 --> 00:08:26.535 align:middle line:84%
is a subset of the set
of avid hanggliders.

00:08:26.535 --> 00:08:31.630 align:middle line:90%


00:08:31.630 --> 00:08:36.320 align:middle line:90%
What set does "every Italian"--

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:38.289 align:middle line:90%
Enrico Flor is Italian--

00:08:38.289 --> 00:08:43.200 align:middle line:84%
what set does "every
Italian" refer to,

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:45.780 align:middle line:84%
if we're going to let noun
phrases refer to sets?

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:47.560 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:50.680 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Either every
person such that that person

00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:55.573 align:middle line:84%
has Italian citizenship
or Italian ancestry?

00:08:55.573 --> 00:08:57.740 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah, so
you're being very careful.

00:08:57.740 --> 00:08:59.520 align:middle line:84%
But yes, so somehow
we'll have to define

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:00.520 align:middle line:90%
what we mean by Italian.

00:09:00.520 --> 00:09:01.062 align:middle line:90%
You're right.

00:09:01.062 --> 00:09:01.960 align:middle line:90%
That's right.

00:09:01.960 --> 00:09:05.020 align:middle line:84%
And then we'll end up
with a list of Italians.

00:09:05.020 --> 00:09:07.510 align:middle line:90%
And then that'll be the set.

00:09:07.510 --> 00:09:10.570 align:middle line:84%
So similarly, if you say "Every
Italian is an avid hangglider,"

00:09:10.570 --> 00:09:13.670 align:middle line:84%
what we mean is it's kind of
like "The 24.900 TAs are avid

00:09:13.670 --> 00:09:14.350 align:middle line:90%
hanggliders."

00:09:14.350 --> 00:09:16.150 align:middle line:84%
Everyone in this list
is also in this list.

00:09:16.150 --> 00:09:17.920 align:middle line:84%
Everyone in this set
is also in this set.

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:20.020 align:middle line:90%
That's what that means.

00:09:20.020 --> 00:09:22.587 align:middle line:84%
So "every Italian" will
have a list of Italians--

00:09:22.587 --> 00:09:23.920 align:middle line:90%
that's how the list could start.

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:29.050 align:middle line:84%
And you're saying
everybody in that

00:09:29.050 --> 00:09:32.120 align:middle line:90%
list is an avid hangglider.

00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:35.282 align:middle line:90%
OK, so far, so good.

00:09:35.282 --> 00:09:36.240 align:middle line:90%
How about "No Italian?"

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:36.990 align:middle line:90%
What set is that?

00:09:36.990 --> 00:09:39.790 align:middle line:90%


00:09:39.790 --> 00:09:40.960 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:43.210 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It's like if you
took the set of all people,

00:09:43.210 --> 00:09:45.280 align:middle line:84%
but you took out
all of the Italians.

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:46.988 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Subtracted the Italians.

00:09:46.988 --> 00:09:49.568 align:middle line:90%


00:09:49.568 --> 00:09:51.860 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It would be the
partition of all people who's

00:09:51.860 --> 00:09:53.135 align:middle line:90%
not Italian.

00:09:53.135 --> 00:09:54.760 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: But
if it means that--

00:09:54.760 --> 00:09:56.500 align:middle line:90%
no, that's a nice idea.

00:09:56.500 --> 00:09:59.553 align:middle line:84%
So we'll take the set of people,
and we'll subtract from it

00:09:59.553 --> 00:10:00.970 align:middle line:84%
all of the Italians,
everybody who

00:10:00.970 --> 00:10:02.410 align:middle line:90%
was on the list of Italians.

00:10:02.410 --> 00:10:05.170 align:middle line:84%
But then what is "No Italian
is an avid hangglider"

00:10:05.170 --> 00:10:05.890 align:middle line:90%
going to mean?

00:10:05.890 --> 00:10:08.610 align:middle line:90%


00:10:08.610 --> 00:10:09.352 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:10:09.352 --> 00:10:11.310 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Just means that
of the list of people

00:10:11.310 --> 00:10:13.560 align:middle line:84%
that you identify as
Italians, none of them

00:10:13.560 --> 00:10:15.997 align:middle line:84%
will be found in the
list of avid hanggliders.

00:10:15.997 --> 00:10:17.830 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So we
want it to mean that.

00:10:17.830 --> 00:10:19.260 align:middle line:90%
But if it meant what Raquel--

00:10:19.260 --> 00:10:21.498 align:middle line:84%
if no Italian meant what
Raquel wanted it to make--

00:10:21.498 --> 00:10:23.040 align:middle line:84%
I'm sorry, Raquel,
I'm picking on you

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:25.410 align:middle line:84%
because you were smart enough
to make a suggestion-- it

00:10:25.410 --> 00:10:29.858 align:middle line:84%
would mean everyone who is not
Italian is an avid hangglider.

00:10:29.858 --> 00:10:32.400 align:middle line:84%
And that's not what "No Italian
is an avid hangglider" means,

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:33.270 align:middle line:90%
I think.

00:10:33.270 --> 00:10:34.785 align:middle line:84%
Sorry, what were
you going to say?

00:10:34.785 --> 00:10:35.190 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:10:35.190 --> 00:10:36.482 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Say that, yes.

00:10:36.482 --> 00:10:39.000 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, I'm sorry, I should
start calling on you

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:40.922 align:middle line:90%
guys instead of-- yes, Raquel?

00:10:40.922 --> 00:10:42.630 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: If there is
an avid hangglider,

00:10:42.630 --> 00:10:46.230 align:middle line:84%
it has to be part
of the subtracted--

00:10:46.230 --> 00:10:48.450 align:middle line:84%
like the set that
doesn't have Italian--

00:10:48.450 --> 00:10:51.210 align:middle line:84%
like every single one must, but
if there is going to be one,

00:10:51.210 --> 00:10:52.410 align:middle line:90%
it better be in that.

00:10:52.410 --> 00:10:54.202 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: All
the other hang gliders

00:10:54.202 --> 00:10:57.030 align:middle line:84%
have to be in the complement
set of the set of Italians,

00:10:57.030 --> 00:10:58.800 align:middle line:90%
something like that.

00:10:58.800 --> 00:10:59.630 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:10:59.630 --> 00:11:00.930 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, Joseph?

00:11:00.930 --> 00:11:03.180 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Instead of Italians,
instead of hanggliders,

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:04.523 align:middle line:90%
the intersection is a null set.

00:11:04.523 --> 00:11:06.690 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah, so
I think what we're saying,

00:11:06.690 --> 00:11:09.330 align:middle line:84%
what we're getting to and
I think you said this more

00:11:09.330 --> 00:11:13.260 align:middle line:84%
or less the same way, every
other set that we've talked

00:11:13.260 --> 00:11:16.590 align:middle line:84%
about, when we said "The 24.900
TAs are avid hanggliders,"

00:11:16.590 --> 00:11:19.110 align:middle line:84%
we said, yeah, there's a set
of 24.900 TAs and there's a set

00:11:19.110 --> 00:11:21.930 align:middle line:84%
of avid hanggliders, and
the first set is contained

00:11:21.930 --> 00:11:23.130 align:middle line:90%
in the second set.

00:11:23.130 --> 00:11:24.210 align:middle line:90%
That's what that means.

00:11:24.210 --> 00:11:26.677 align:middle line:84%
And every Italian is
an avid hangglider--

00:11:26.677 --> 00:11:29.010 align:middle line:84%
there's a set of Italians and
a set of avid hanggliders,

00:11:29.010 --> 00:11:31.410 align:middle line:84%
and the first set is
contained in the second set.

00:11:31.410 --> 00:11:35.280 align:middle line:84%
But "no," as in
"no Italian," makes

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:38.280 align:middle line:84%
you have the sets interact with
each other in a different way.

00:11:38.280 --> 00:11:42.420 align:middle line:84%
It says this first
set is not contained.

00:11:42.420 --> 00:11:45.520 align:middle line:84%
Nothing in this first set is
contained in the second set.

00:11:45.520 --> 00:11:47.665 align:middle line:84%
So this is a popular
way of talking

00:11:47.665 --> 00:11:50.040 align:middle line:84%
about the meanings of what
are called quantifiers-- words

00:11:50.040 --> 00:11:52.140 align:middle line:90%
like "every" and "no."

00:11:52.140 --> 00:11:53.250 align:middle line:90%
So it's not the null set.

00:11:53.250 --> 00:11:54.990 align:middle line:84%
It's not a set
containing no Italian.

00:11:54.990 --> 00:11:58.570 align:middle line:90%


00:11:58.570 --> 00:12:01.653 align:middle line:84%
A popular way of talking about
the meanings of these kinds

00:12:01.653 --> 00:12:03.570 align:middle line:84%
of expressions-- these
are called quantifiers,

00:12:03.570 --> 00:12:07.620 align:middle line:84%
these expressions which do
fancier things with sets than

00:12:07.620 --> 00:12:11.172 align:middle line:84%
just say, oh yeah, this
set is inside this set--

00:12:11.172 --> 00:12:12.630 align:middle line:84%
a popular way of
talking about them

00:12:12.630 --> 00:12:16.020 align:middle line:84%
is to say they do
fancier things with sets.

00:12:16.020 --> 00:12:18.660 align:middle line:84%
They allow you to do
more interesting things

00:12:18.660 --> 00:12:20.970 align:middle line:84%
with set interaction
than just say this set is

00:12:20.970 --> 00:12:22.380 align:middle line:90%
a subset of that set.

00:12:22.380 --> 00:12:23.380 align:middle line:90%
That's what they do.

00:12:23.380 --> 00:12:25.680 align:middle line:90%
That's what they're for.

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:28.847 align:middle line:84%
Quantifiers have a bunch
of interesting properties.

00:12:28.847 --> 00:12:30.180 align:middle line:90%
They're weird in other ways too.

00:12:30.180 --> 00:12:33.060 align:middle line:84%
So, for example, there
is something called

00:12:33.060 --> 00:12:35.760 align:middle line:90%
the Law of Contradiction.

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:39.180 align:middle line:84%
If-- the Law of Contradiction
says if you take two predicates

00:12:39.180 --> 00:12:43.660 align:middle line:84%
that are contradictory, like
"be inside" and "be outside,"

00:12:43.660 --> 00:12:47.910 align:middle line:84%
if you join me in imagining that
it's not possible to be both

00:12:47.910 --> 00:12:51.000 align:middle line:90%
inside and outside--

00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:52.553 align:middle line:84%
and I can see some
of you thinking

00:12:52.553 --> 00:12:54.970 align:middle line:84%
of alternative ways of thinking
about the world in which--

00:12:54.970 --> 00:12:58.000 align:middle line:84%
but suppose I'm halfway
inside and halfway outside.

00:12:58.000 --> 00:12:59.760 align:middle line:90%
Which am I?

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:02.035 align:middle line:84%
I'm in the Department of
Linguistics and Philosophy,

00:13:02.035 --> 00:13:03.910 align:middle line:84%
so I'm used to hearing
people talk like that.

00:13:03.910 --> 00:13:06.480 align:middle line:90%
But please stop.

00:13:06.480 --> 00:13:09.095 align:middle line:84%
Just imagine that you're either
inside or you are outside

00:13:09.095 --> 00:13:09.720 align:middle line:90%
and that's all.

00:13:09.720 --> 00:13:12.000 align:middle line:84%
And imagine that Paul
is a single person.

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:14.700 align:middle line:84%
There's only one person
on Earth named Paul.

00:13:14.700 --> 00:13:21.930 align:middle line:84%
So then "Paul is inside and
Paul is outside" cannot be true.

00:13:21.930 --> 00:13:23.560 align:middle line:90%
That's the Law of Contradiction.

00:13:23.560 --> 00:13:27.150 align:middle line:84%
So if you have two predicates
that are opposites of each

00:13:27.150 --> 00:13:29.760 align:middle line:84%
other, cannot both be
true of a single person,

00:13:29.760 --> 00:13:33.360 align:middle line:84%
then if you apply them
to a single person,

00:13:33.360 --> 00:13:35.080 align:middle line:84%
then the sentence
has to be false.

00:13:35.080 --> 00:13:36.960 align:middle line:84%
That's what that
first example is.

00:13:36.960 --> 00:13:39.600 align:middle line:84%
It's an example of the
Law of Contradiction.

00:13:39.600 --> 00:13:42.390 align:middle line:84%
But there are quantifiers
like "several Americans,"

00:13:42.390 --> 00:13:45.120 align:middle line:84%
which just flagrantly violate
the Law of Contradiction.

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:47.370 align:middle line:84%
So "Several Americans are
inside and several Americans

00:13:47.370 --> 00:13:48.660 align:middle line:90%
are outside"--

00:13:48.660 --> 00:13:49.800 align:middle line:90%
fine.

00:13:49.800 --> 00:13:50.970 align:middle line:90%
No problem.

00:13:50.970 --> 00:13:52.680 align:middle line:84%
And you don't have
to play games with

00:13:52.680 --> 00:13:55.110 align:middle line:84%
whether if you're halfway
inside or halfway outside

00:13:55.110 --> 00:13:59.488 align:middle line:84%
or you're inside the building
but you're outside the campus

00:13:59.488 --> 00:14:00.030 align:middle line:90%
or something.

00:14:00.030 --> 00:14:01.320 align:middle line:90%
Just forget about all that.

00:14:01.320 --> 00:14:06.320 align:middle line:90%


00:14:06.320 --> 00:14:08.540 align:middle line:84%
There are also
quantifiers that fail

00:14:08.540 --> 00:14:11.990 align:middle line:84%
what's called the Law
of the Excluded Middle.

00:14:11.990 --> 00:14:13.880 align:middle line:84%
The Law of the
Excluded Middle says

00:14:13.880 --> 00:14:17.930 align:middle line:84%
if you have two predicates
like "be under 6 feet tall"

00:14:17.930 --> 00:14:22.260 align:middle line:84%
and "be over 5 feet
tall," then a sentence

00:14:22.260 --> 00:14:24.240 align:middle line:84%
like "Takashi is
under 6 feet tall

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:29.230 align:middle line:84%
or Takashi is over 5 feet
tall" has to be true.

00:14:29.230 --> 00:14:32.700 align:middle line:84%
We don't have to measure Takashi
to find out whether that's true

00:14:32.700 --> 00:14:34.530 align:middle line:84%
because the set
of people who are

00:14:34.530 --> 00:14:38.790 align:middle line:84%
under 6 feet tall and the set of
people who are over 5 feet tall

00:14:38.790 --> 00:14:40.240 align:middle line:90%
overlap.

00:14:40.240 --> 00:14:42.030 align:middle line:84%
If you have both of
those sets together,

00:14:42.030 --> 00:14:44.070 align:middle line:90%
they cover all people.

00:14:44.070 --> 00:14:47.730 align:middle line:84%
Any height that someone
is, they are at least one

00:14:47.730 --> 00:14:49.980 align:middle line:84%
of those two things,
possibly both.

00:14:49.980 --> 00:14:53.320 align:middle line:84%
Someone who's between 5 feet
tall and 6 feet tall is both.

00:14:53.320 --> 00:14:56.410 align:middle line:84%
And someone who's
under 5 is under 6,

00:14:56.410 --> 00:15:00.590 align:middle line:84%
and someone who's over
6 is, well, over 5.

00:15:00.590 --> 00:15:01.760 align:middle line:90%
That sound right?

00:15:01.760 --> 00:15:06.290 align:middle line:84%
These are two predicates
such that here's 5 feet tall.

00:15:06.290 --> 00:15:07.800 align:middle line:90%
Here's 6 feet tall.

00:15:07.800 --> 00:15:10.670 align:middle line:84%
We're talking about people
who are under 6 feet tall

00:15:10.670 --> 00:15:13.190 align:middle line:84%
and also the people who
are over 5 feet tall.

00:15:13.190 --> 00:15:15.500 align:middle line:90%
Well, we've covered everybody.

00:15:15.500 --> 00:15:17.360 align:middle line:84%
So we don't have
to look at Takashi.

00:15:17.360 --> 00:15:20.592 align:middle line:84%
We don't have to
know who Takashi is.

00:15:20.592 --> 00:15:21.800 align:middle line:90%
That first sentence is true--

00:15:21.800 --> 00:15:27.290 align:middle line:84%
Takashi is under 6 feet tall
or Takashi is over 5 feet tall.

00:15:27.290 --> 00:15:30.590 align:middle line:84%
But the second sentence
is false, or at least

00:15:30.590 --> 00:15:32.100 align:middle line:90%
doesn't have to be true.

00:15:32.100 --> 00:15:34.760 align:middle line:84%
So "All Japanese men
are under 6 feet tall

00:15:34.760 --> 00:15:39.440 align:middle line:84%
or all Japanese men
are over 5 feet tall."

00:15:39.440 --> 00:15:41.090 align:middle line:90%
That could be false.

00:15:41.090 --> 00:15:43.805 align:middle line:84%
Can somebody name a context
in which that would be false?

00:15:43.805 --> 00:15:47.655 align:middle line:90%


00:15:47.655 --> 00:15:49.280 align:middle line:84%
I'm asking you to do
it because I can't

00:15:49.280 --> 00:15:50.630 align:middle line:90%
do math while standing up here.

00:15:50.630 --> 00:15:51.950 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, Joseph.

00:15:51.950 --> 00:15:55.483 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: There happened
to be a particularly tall--

00:15:55.483 --> 00:15:58.360 align:middle line:84%
someone who's over 6 feet,
or particularly short

00:15:58.360 --> 00:16:01.677 align:middle line:84%
under 5 feet [INAUDIBLE],, and
the population of Japanese men

00:16:01.677 --> 00:16:02.710 align:middle line:90%
are [INAUDIBLE].

00:16:02.710 --> 00:16:04.822 align:middle line:90%


00:16:04.822 --> 00:16:06.530 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah,
there we go, good.

00:16:06.530 --> 00:16:08.810 align:middle line:84%
So as long as you have at
least one Japanese man who's

00:16:08.810 --> 00:16:10.910 align:middle line:84%
over 6 feet tall and at
least one Japanese man who

00:16:10.910 --> 00:16:13.160 align:middle line:84%
is under 5 feet tall, the
rest of the Japanese men

00:16:13.160 --> 00:16:14.580 align:middle line:90%
can all do whatever they want.

00:16:14.580 --> 00:16:16.970 align:middle line:90%
The sentence will be false.

00:16:16.970 --> 00:16:18.110 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:16:18.110 --> 00:16:21.080 align:middle line:84%
So quantifier
phrases, quantifiers,

00:16:21.080 --> 00:16:23.690 align:middle line:84%
quantification expressions,
like "all Japanese men"

00:16:23.690 --> 00:16:28.610 align:middle line:84%
or "some Italians"
or "no Italians,"

00:16:28.610 --> 00:16:36.410 align:middle line:84%
fail these otherwise
reliable generalizations

00:16:36.410 --> 00:16:40.100 align:middle line:90%
about sentences.

00:16:40.100 --> 00:16:43.070 align:middle line:84%
So expressions like "no
Turks" or "several Americans"

00:16:43.070 --> 00:16:44.810 align:middle line:84%
or "all Italians"
or "most Ukrainians"

00:16:44.810 --> 00:16:46.380 align:middle line:90%
don't refer to sets of people.

00:16:46.380 --> 00:16:47.130 align:middle line:90%
What do they mean?

00:16:47.130 --> 00:16:50.970 align:middle line:90%
Well, what they do is they--

00:16:50.970 --> 00:16:53.960 align:middle line:84%
already said this-- these words
at the beginning, like "no"

00:16:53.960 --> 00:16:59.540 align:middle line:84%
or "several" or "all" or
"most," are doing cool set

00:16:59.540 --> 00:17:03.440 align:middle line:84%
theoretic things with the set
that they are combining with.

00:17:03.440 --> 00:17:07.190 align:middle line:84%
So the set of Turks or Americans
or Italians or Ukrainians,

00:17:07.190 --> 00:17:09.829 align:middle line:84%
they're causing that set to
interact in interesting ways

00:17:09.829 --> 00:17:12.387 align:middle line:84%
with a set that's determined
by the rest of the sentence.

00:17:12.387 --> 00:17:13.970 align:middle line:84%
In order to talk
about this, let me do

00:17:13.970 --> 00:17:18.488 align:middle line:90%
a quick review of set theory.

00:17:18.488 --> 00:17:20.530 align:middle line:84%
You're not going to need
to very much that theory

00:17:20.530 --> 00:17:21.859 align:middle line:90%
in order to do this.

00:17:21.859 --> 00:17:24.069 align:middle line:84%
I tell you this because I
don't very much set theory,

00:17:24.069 --> 00:17:27.970 align:middle line:84%
and I can do this, so
I'll just show you what

00:17:27.970 --> 00:17:30.980 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to show you here.

00:17:30.980 --> 00:17:34.270 align:middle line:90%
Here are two sets, pi and phi--

00:17:34.270 --> 00:17:35.470 align:middle line:90%
a Venn diagram.

00:17:35.470 --> 00:17:37.750 align:middle line:84%
It's hopefully not
too unfamiliar.

00:17:37.750 --> 00:17:41.590 align:middle line:84%
Two sets, pi and phi,
which have an overlap

00:17:41.590 --> 00:17:44.290 align:middle line:84%
that contains D and F,
and then there are also

00:17:44.290 --> 00:17:47.178 align:middle line:84%
things that are only in pi
and not in phi, like A and B.

00:17:47.178 --> 00:17:49.720 align:middle line:84%
And there are some things that
are only in phi and not in pi,

00:17:49.720 --> 00:17:51.520 align:middle line:90%
like C and E. That sound right?

00:17:51.520 --> 00:17:55.020 align:middle line:84%
People are all familiar
with Venn diagrams.

00:17:55.020 --> 00:17:59.970 align:middle line:84%
We say that D and F is the
intersection of pi and phi,

00:17:59.970 --> 00:18:04.140 align:middle line:84%
and that little hoop
is the thing that you

00:18:04.140 --> 00:18:07.380 align:middle line:90%
use to express intersection.

00:18:07.380 --> 00:18:12.997 align:middle line:84%
And we say that A, B, C, D, and
F is the union of pi and phi,

00:18:12.997 --> 00:18:15.330 align:middle line:84%
where I have, for some reason,
used the word "of" twice.

00:18:15.330 --> 00:18:18.900 align:middle line:84%
I'll try to fix that
before I put up the slides.

00:18:18.900 --> 00:18:21.450 align:middle line:84%
And the little thing that
looks more or less like a U

00:18:21.450 --> 00:18:24.510 align:middle line:90%
is the expression for union.

00:18:24.510 --> 00:18:28.410 align:middle line:84%
We also say that A, B,
and D is a subset of pi,

00:18:28.410 --> 00:18:31.515 align:middle line:84%
and there's a symbol that's
used sometimes for subsets.

00:18:31.515 --> 00:18:34.560 align:middle line:90%


00:18:34.560 --> 00:18:39.970 align:middle line:84%
None of this seems alarming--
subsets, union, intersection?

00:18:39.970 --> 00:18:42.285 align:middle line:84%
OK, so here's the
popular answer--

00:18:42.285 --> 00:18:44.410 align:middle line:84%
which we've now already
gone through for quantifier

00:18:44.410 --> 00:18:47.590 align:middle line:84%
meaning-- what we say is
when you're saying something

00:18:47.590 --> 00:18:52.120 align:middle line:84%
like "All Americans eat junk
food" is you are asserting

00:18:52.120 --> 00:18:54.040 align:middle line:84%
a relation between
the set of Americans

00:18:54.040 --> 00:18:56.882 align:middle line:90%
and the set of junk food eaters.

00:18:56.882 --> 00:18:58.840 align:middle line:84%
You're saying something
about what happens when

00:18:58.840 --> 00:19:02.590 align:middle line:90%
you intersect those two sets.

00:19:02.590 --> 00:19:05.530 align:middle line:84%
And depending on what
quantifier you're using,

00:19:05.530 --> 00:19:07.080 align:middle line:84%
you're making
different assertions

00:19:07.080 --> 00:19:08.830 align:middle line:84%
about the relation
between these two sets.

00:19:08.830 --> 00:19:14.206 align:middle line:84%
So "all" says set number one
is a subset of set number two--

00:19:14.206 --> 00:19:15.395 align:middle line:90%
what some say.

00:19:15.395 --> 00:19:18.125 align:middle line:90%


00:19:18.125 --> 00:19:19.085 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, [INAUDIBLE]?

00:19:19.085 --> 00:19:20.960 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The intersection
is not a null set.

00:19:20.960 --> 00:19:23.180 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah, the
intersection is nonempty.

00:19:23.180 --> 00:19:24.930 align:middle line:84%
So there are things
that are in both sets.

00:19:24.930 --> 00:19:28.070 align:middle line:90%


00:19:28.070 --> 00:19:31.730 align:middle line:84%
"No" says the intersection of
set one and set two is empty--

00:19:31.730 --> 00:19:33.410 align:middle line:90%
"No Americans eat nattoo"--

00:19:33.410 --> 00:19:35.110 align:middle line:90%
are people familiar with nattoo?

00:19:35.110 --> 00:19:36.860 align:middle line:84%
Have we talked about
nattoo in this class?

00:19:36.860 --> 00:19:39.560 align:middle line:84%
Yes, some of us are
from nattoo cultures.

00:19:39.560 --> 00:19:43.670 align:middle line:90%
Nattoo is a Japanese food.

00:19:43.670 --> 00:19:46.460 align:middle line:84%
It's very good, and
it's good for you.

00:19:46.460 --> 00:19:48.985 align:middle line:84%
It's high in protein, but it's
one of those kinds of food--

00:19:48.985 --> 00:19:51.110 align:middle line:84%
I think a lot of cultures
have something like this.

00:19:51.110 --> 00:19:54.050 align:middle line:84%
It's a kind of food one
of the points of which

00:19:54.050 --> 00:19:56.180 align:middle line:84%
is to feed it to
outsiders so that you

00:19:56.180 --> 00:19:59.010 align:middle line:90%
can watch and be amused.

00:19:59.010 --> 00:20:01.790 align:middle line:84%
So this happened to me
when I was living in Japan.

00:20:01.790 --> 00:20:05.330 align:middle line:84%
We were visiting some the family
of a Japanese friend of mine,

00:20:05.330 --> 00:20:07.622 align:middle line:84%
and the mother made me
up a big dish of nattoo

00:20:07.622 --> 00:20:09.830 align:middle line:84%
and they put it in front of
me, and the entire family

00:20:09.830 --> 00:20:11.720 align:middle line:90%
gathered around to watch.

00:20:11.720 --> 00:20:13.430 align:middle line:84%
So you see what is
he going to do?--

00:20:13.430 --> 00:20:17.010 align:middle line:90%
Nattoo is fermented soybeans.

00:20:17.010 --> 00:20:23.660 align:middle line:84%
So the soybeans are covered
in a thin layer of slime,

00:20:23.660 --> 00:20:27.680 align:middle line:84%
and then you mix this up
with rice and often mustard

00:20:27.680 --> 00:20:29.000 align:middle line:90%
and soy sauce.

00:20:29.000 --> 00:20:29.750 align:middle line:90%
It's really tasty.

00:20:29.750 --> 00:20:31.542 align:middle line:84%
It's really good, but
it's also very messy.

00:20:31.542 --> 00:20:34.042 align:middle line:84%
So if you're like me and
you have facial hair,

00:20:34.042 --> 00:20:35.750 align:middle line:84%
by the time you're
done, your entire face

00:20:35.750 --> 00:20:38.180 align:middle line:84%
is covered in
nattoo slime, so you

00:20:38.180 --> 00:20:39.960 align:middle line:84%
have to go take a
shower after you eat.

00:20:39.960 --> 00:20:42.110 align:middle line:90%
But it's good for you.

00:20:42.110 --> 00:20:43.460 align:middle line:90%
Tasty stuff.

00:20:43.460 --> 00:20:45.380 align:middle line:84%
So it's not true that
no Americans eat nattoo,

00:20:45.380 --> 00:20:46.255 align:middle line:90%
but it's grammatical.

00:20:46.255 --> 00:20:48.147 align:middle line:84%
And what it means is
the set of Americans--

00:20:48.147 --> 00:20:49.730 align:middle line:84%
you're looking at
the set of Americans

00:20:49.730 --> 00:20:51.397 align:middle line:84%
and the set of nattoo
eaters, and you're

00:20:51.397 --> 00:20:53.940 align:middle line:84%
saying the intersection
of those sets is empty.

00:20:53.940 --> 00:20:57.770 align:middle line:84%
There's nothing that's
in both of these sets.

00:20:57.770 --> 00:21:00.740 align:middle line:84%
OK, I don't know whether
we have a food like that

00:21:00.740 --> 00:21:03.427 align:middle line:84%
in our culture, a food
that you feed to people

00:21:03.427 --> 00:21:05.510 align:middle line:84%
in order to be amused by
their attempts to eat it.

00:21:05.510 --> 00:21:07.310 align:middle line:84%
This is something
to think about.

00:21:07.310 --> 00:21:08.780 align:middle line:84%
I can tell you that the
Tagalog version of it though.

00:21:08.780 --> 00:21:11.238 align:middle line:84%
I'm sorry, I'll start talking
about sets again in a second.

00:21:11.238 --> 00:21:13.910 align:middle line:84%
The Tagalog version of this
is something called balut.

00:21:13.910 --> 00:21:15.800 align:middle line:84%
Have I talked about
it in this class?

00:21:15.800 --> 00:21:16.730 align:middle line:90%
You've had balut too?

00:21:16.730 --> 00:21:18.020 align:middle line:90%
You're an adventurous person.

00:21:18.020 --> 00:21:21.485 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I have
not had [INAUDIBLE]..

00:21:21.485 --> 00:21:22.897 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Not balut, OK.

00:21:22.897 --> 00:21:24.230 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: --a whole other level.

00:21:24.230 --> 00:21:27.860 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah,
balut is a duck's egg

00:21:27.860 --> 00:21:29.720 align:middle line:84%
that has been allowed
to be fertilized.

00:21:29.720 --> 00:21:31.345 align:middle line:84%
It's a popular food
in the Philippines,

00:21:31.345 --> 00:21:32.813 align:middle line:90%
so people sell it in the street.

00:21:32.813 --> 00:21:34.730 align:middle line:84%
You have balut sellers
who walk around saying,

00:21:34.730 --> 00:21:39.170 align:middle line:84%
balut [NON-ENGLISH],, which means
"Balut strengthens your knees."

00:21:39.170 --> 00:21:42.590 align:middle line:84%
That's apparently the
standard thing you say.

00:21:42.590 --> 00:21:46.100 align:middle line:84%
And balut is a duck's egg
that's been fertilized and then

00:21:46.100 --> 00:21:47.738 align:middle line:90%
hardboiled before it hatches.

00:21:47.738 --> 00:21:49.280 align:middle line:84%
So you've got a
hardboiled duck's egg

00:21:49.280 --> 00:21:53.148 align:middle line:84%
with a duck embryo inside,
which you're supposed to eat.

00:21:53.148 --> 00:21:54.690 align:middle line:84%
When I was living
in the Philippines,

00:21:54.690 --> 00:21:56.810 align:middle line:84%
my host brother got
me got me a balut.

00:21:56.810 --> 00:21:58.993 align:middle line:90%
And we were in a dark room.

00:21:58.993 --> 00:22:00.410 align:middle line:84%
He handed me the
balut, and I went

00:22:00.410 --> 00:22:05.270 align:middle line:84%
to turn on the light he
was like, no, just eat it.

00:22:05.270 --> 00:22:08.280 align:middle line:84%
And I unfortunately turned on
the light, so I chickened out.

00:22:08.280 --> 00:22:12.380 align:middle line:84%
I looked at it, and there's a
little duck looking up at me.

00:22:12.380 --> 00:22:14.160 align:middle line:90%
Pretty weird.

00:22:14.160 --> 00:22:17.270 align:middle line:84%
So quantifiers-- so that's
their version of that.

00:22:17.270 --> 00:22:23.300 align:middle line:84%
Quantifiers then-- quantifiers
like all or no or three--

00:22:23.300 --> 00:22:27.470 align:middle line:84%
are saying things about the
interactions of the two sets.

00:22:27.470 --> 00:22:30.230 align:middle line:90%
So "all" says the first set.

00:22:30.230 --> 00:22:34.280 align:middle line:84%
So in a sentence like "All
Americans eat nattoo,"

00:22:34.280 --> 00:22:35.900 align:middle line:84%
the first set is
the set of Americans

00:22:35.900 --> 00:22:38.780 align:middle line:84%
and the second set is
the set of nattoo eaters,

00:22:38.780 --> 00:22:41.540 align:middle line:84%
"all" says the first set is
a subset of the second set.

00:22:41.540 --> 00:22:44.390 align:middle line:84%
"Some" says the intersection
of these sets is nonempty.

00:22:44.390 --> 00:22:47.350 align:middle line:84%
"No" says the intersection
of these sets is empty.

00:22:47.350 --> 00:22:49.850 align:middle line:84%
"Three" says, if you look at
the intersection of these sets,

00:22:49.850 --> 00:22:51.050 align:middle line:90%
you will find three things.

00:22:51.050 --> 00:22:53.403 align:middle line:90%
That has cardinality three.

00:22:53.403 --> 00:22:55.445 align:middle line:84%
That's the kind of meaning
that a quantifier has.

00:22:55.445 --> 00:23:01.120 align:middle line:90%


00:23:01.120 --> 00:23:04.120 align:middle line:84%
If you're already
familiar with set theory,

00:23:04.120 --> 00:23:06.610 align:middle line:84%
it's obviously
interesting to try

00:23:06.610 --> 00:23:10.630 align:middle line:84%
to think about what kinds of
intersections between sets

00:23:10.630 --> 00:23:14.200 align:middle line:90%
quantifiers can state.

00:23:14.200 --> 00:23:18.610 align:middle line:84%
So mathematicians can
get sets to tangle

00:23:18.610 --> 00:23:20.980 align:middle line:84%
with each other in all
kinds of entertaining ways.

00:23:20.980 --> 00:23:23.860 align:middle line:84%
I mean, there are all kinds of
things you can get sets to do.

00:23:23.860 --> 00:23:26.920 align:middle line:84%
It turns out that natural
language quantifiers

00:23:26.920 --> 00:23:31.180 align:middle line:84%
are what's called conservative,
which means that you can always

00:23:31.180 --> 00:23:35.410 align:middle line:84%
replace set number two with the
intersection of set number one

00:23:35.410 --> 00:23:37.630 align:middle line:84%
and set number two and
get the same meaning.

00:23:37.630 --> 00:23:38.297 align:middle line:90%
That is to say--

00:23:38.297 --> 00:23:40.380 align:middle line:84%
maybe I'm about to do this
on the slide, but here,

00:23:40.380 --> 00:23:41.800 align:middle line:90%
we'll do it on the board--

00:23:41.800 --> 00:23:46.672 align:middle line:84%
if this is set number one
and this is set number two,

00:23:46.672 --> 00:23:48.130 align:middle line:84%
I've been talking
about quantifiers

00:23:48.130 --> 00:23:49.505 align:middle line:84%
as though they
tell you something

00:23:49.505 --> 00:23:51.470 align:middle line:90%
about this set and this set.

00:23:51.470 --> 00:23:56.125 align:middle line:84%
But all of the quantifiers can
be stated in terms of this set,

00:23:56.125 --> 00:24:00.550 align:middle line:84%
set number one, and this area
here, the intersection of set

00:24:00.550 --> 00:24:01.960 align:middle line:90%
number one and set number two.

00:24:01.960 --> 00:24:06.880 align:middle line:84%
This part of set
number two is always

00:24:06.880 --> 00:24:09.280 align:middle line:84%
irrelevant to the
meaning of a quantifier.

00:24:09.280 --> 00:24:14.110 align:middle line:84%
That's a fact about natural
language quantifiers.

00:24:14.110 --> 00:24:16.790 align:middle line:90%
And it's not a necessary fact.

00:24:16.790 --> 00:24:19.900 align:middle line:84%
So here's an example, if
I say "All opera singers

00:24:19.900 --> 00:24:22.090 align:middle line:84%
smoke," which I
believe to be false,

00:24:22.090 --> 00:24:25.600 align:middle line:84%
but anyway, I'm making a
claim about the relationship

00:24:25.600 --> 00:24:28.943 align:middle line:84%
between the set of opera
singers and the set of smokers.

00:24:28.943 --> 00:24:30.610 align:middle line:84%
I'm saying that the
set of opera singers

00:24:30.610 --> 00:24:34.320 align:middle line:84%
is completely contained
in the set of smokers.

00:24:34.320 --> 00:24:37.230 align:middle line:84%
But that's essentially
the same thing.

00:24:37.230 --> 00:24:40.320 align:middle line:84%
It is the same thing as saying
that all opera singers are

00:24:40.320 --> 00:24:41.460 align:middle line:90%
opera singers who smoke.

00:24:41.460 --> 00:24:44.370 align:middle line:84%
That is, opera singers are all
in the set of smoking opera

00:24:44.370 --> 00:24:46.020 align:middle line:90%
singers.

00:24:46.020 --> 00:24:50.790 align:middle line:84%
We don't care about smokers
who are not opera singers when

00:24:50.790 --> 00:24:54.210 align:middle line:84%
we are thinking about the
meaning of "All opera singers

00:24:54.210 --> 00:24:55.390 align:middle line:90%
smoke."

00:24:55.390 --> 00:24:57.450 align:middle line:84%
So if I tell you "All
opera singers smoke"

00:24:57.450 --> 00:25:00.570 align:middle line:84%
and you begin to talk to me
about someone who smokes and is

00:25:00.570 --> 00:25:04.270 align:middle line:84%
not an opera singer, which
may be fun to talk about that,

00:25:04.270 --> 00:25:06.760 align:middle line:84%
but it's not relevant to
the truth of my claim.

00:25:06.760 --> 00:25:10.230 align:middle line:84%
The truth of my claim is only
about the set of opera singers

00:25:10.230 --> 00:25:12.258 align:middle line:84%
and the set of
smoking opera singers.

00:25:12.258 --> 00:25:13.800 align:middle line:84%
It says that the
set of opera singers

00:25:13.800 --> 00:25:16.950 align:middle line:84%
is completely contained in the
set of smoking opera singers.

00:25:16.950 --> 00:25:18.760 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:25:18.760 --> 00:25:21.060 align:middle line:84%
So if I say "All opera singers
smoke" and you tell me,

00:25:21.060 --> 00:25:23.550 align:middle line:84%
"Oh no, Arnold
Schwarzenegger smokes,

00:25:23.550 --> 00:25:27.210 align:middle line:84%
and he's not an opera singer,"
you haven't contradicted me.

00:25:27.210 --> 00:25:28.080 align:middle line:90%
It doesn't matter.

00:25:28.080 --> 00:25:31.860 align:middle line:90%


00:25:31.860 --> 00:25:33.350 align:middle line:84%
You can imagine
quantifiers which

00:25:33.350 --> 00:25:34.438 align:middle line:90%
would not be conservative.

00:25:34.438 --> 00:25:35.480 align:middle line:90%
So I've just made one up.

00:25:35.480 --> 00:25:37.310 align:middle line:90%
Here's a quantifier, "glorp."

00:25:37.310 --> 00:25:41.480 align:middle line:84%
It says, if you add up all of
the things in set number one

00:25:41.480 --> 00:25:44.030 align:middle line:84%
and set number two, so the
union of set number one

00:25:44.030 --> 00:25:46.490 align:middle line:84%
and set number two
has cardinality three.

00:25:46.490 --> 00:25:48.050 align:middle line:90%
I just made up glorp.

00:25:48.050 --> 00:25:49.670 align:middle line:90%
I'll give you an example.

00:25:49.670 --> 00:25:52.910 align:middle line:84%
It would be true, in this
picture, that glorp circles are

00:25:52.910 --> 00:25:57.900 align:middle line:84%
red because if you add
up the number of circles

00:25:57.900 --> 00:26:02.690 align:middle line:84%
and the number of red
things, the total is three.

00:26:02.690 --> 00:26:06.150 align:middle line:84%
There are two circles
and one red thing.

00:26:06.150 --> 00:26:09.740 align:middle line:84%
So if there were a
quantifier glorp,

00:26:09.740 --> 00:26:11.900 align:middle line:84%
then you could say
that glorp circles

00:26:11.900 --> 00:26:14.600 align:middle line:90%
are red in this picture.

00:26:14.600 --> 00:26:17.720 align:middle line:84%
There is no quantifier
glorp, not in English and not

00:26:17.720 --> 00:26:19.820 align:middle line:84%
in any language on Earth
as far as we can tell.

00:26:19.820 --> 00:26:22.910 align:middle line:84%
So quantifiers don't ever
do this kind of thing.

00:26:22.910 --> 00:26:24.890 align:middle line:84%
And this would be a
nonconservative quantifier

00:26:24.890 --> 00:26:27.650 align:middle line:84%
because in order
to evaluate glorp,

00:26:27.650 --> 00:26:30.350 align:middle line:84%
you would have to look not just
at the part of set number two

00:26:30.350 --> 00:26:33.440 align:middle line:84%
that's intersecting
with set number one,

00:26:33.440 --> 00:26:36.020 align:middle line:84%
but also at the part
of set number two

00:26:36.020 --> 00:26:38.330 align:middle line:84%
that I've hatched out
here, the part that's

00:26:38.330 --> 00:26:39.860 align:middle line:90%
not part of set number one.

00:26:39.860 --> 00:26:42.170 align:middle line:84%
So this is a
nonconservative quantifier,

00:26:42.170 --> 00:26:44.000 align:middle line:90%
and there aren't any.

00:26:44.000 --> 00:26:44.780 align:middle line:90%
That's the point.

00:26:44.780 --> 00:26:49.360 align:middle line:90%


00:26:49.360 --> 00:26:51.870 align:middle line:90%
OK, good.

00:26:51.870 --> 00:26:55.500 align:middle line:90%
So just practice some more--

00:26:55.500 --> 00:26:59.130 align:middle line:90%
so "all Brazilians love soccer."

00:26:59.130 --> 00:27:01.890 align:middle line:84%
There's this danger when
you do simple sentences

00:27:01.890 --> 00:27:04.740 align:middle line:84%
with qualifiers in them that you
will find yourself trafficking

00:27:04.740 --> 00:27:05.850 align:middle line:90%
in stereotypes.

00:27:05.850 --> 00:27:08.127 align:middle line:84%
This is why I like
to use examples

00:27:08.127 --> 00:27:10.710 align:middle line:84%
like "All opera singers smoke,"
which there isn't a stereotype

00:27:10.710 --> 00:27:14.290 align:middle line:90%
about as far as I know.

00:27:14.290 --> 00:27:16.590 align:middle line:84%
So, of course, it's not
true that all Brazilians

00:27:16.590 --> 00:27:17.100 align:middle line:90%
love soccer.

00:27:17.100 --> 00:27:19.050 align:middle line:90%
It might almost be true.

00:27:19.050 --> 00:27:23.010 align:middle line:84%
It says there's a relation
between the set of Brazilians

00:27:23.010 --> 00:27:25.140 align:middle line:84%
and the set of people
who love soccer--

00:27:25.140 --> 00:27:27.930 align:middle line:84%
namely, the first set is a
subset of the second set.

00:27:27.930 --> 00:27:30.660 align:middle line:84%
If you look at the set of
people who love soccer,

00:27:30.660 --> 00:27:32.820 align:middle line:84%
inside that set,
you will find all

00:27:32.820 --> 00:27:35.190 align:middle line:84%
of the Brazilians, the
people in the Brazilian set.

00:27:35.190 --> 00:27:38.770 align:middle line:90%


00:27:38.770 --> 00:27:42.100 align:middle line:84%
Let's talk a little more
about how we get these sets,

00:27:42.100 --> 00:27:44.390 align:middle line:84%
just to be slightly
more formal about it.

00:27:44.390 --> 00:27:47.230 align:middle line:84%
So what we'll say is the
set of Brazilians-- maybe

00:27:47.230 --> 00:27:48.430 align:middle line:90%
that's not such a mystery.

00:27:48.430 --> 00:27:50.050 align:middle line:90%
So all Brazilians love soccer--

00:27:50.050 --> 00:27:54.110 align:middle line:84%
you're taking the set from
the rest of the noun phrase.

00:27:54.110 --> 00:27:57.457 align:middle line:84%
So "All Brazilians
love soccer"--

00:27:57.457 --> 00:27:59.290 align:middle line:84%
the first set is just
the set of Brazilians,

00:27:59.290 --> 00:28:02.110 align:middle line:84%
the thing that comes after all
that's part of the noun phrase

00:28:02.110 --> 00:28:03.640 align:middle line:90%
there.

00:28:03.640 --> 00:28:07.120 align:middle line:84%
If it were more extensive
than just Brazilians, then

00:28:07.120 --> 00:28:08.210 align:middle line:90%
that set might change.

00:28:08.210 --> 00:28:11.410 align:middle line:84%
So if I said something like "All
female Brazilians love soccer,"

00:28:11.410 --> 00:28:15.010 align:middle line:84%
"All Brazilians from
São Paulo" love soccer,

00:28:15.010 --> 00:28:18.200 align:middle line:84%
we'd have a different set,
Brazilians from São Paulo.

00:28:18.200 --> 00:28:19.630 align:middle line:90%
And then the second set--

00:28:19.630 --> 00:28:23.680 align:middle line:84%
what we're doing is we're
taking the set of things x such

00:28:23.680 --> 00:28:25.390 align:middle line:90%
that x loves soccer.

00:28:25.390 --> 00:28:28.270 align:middle line:84%
We're getting that
set by replacing

00:28:28.270 --> 00:28:31.370 align:middle line:84%
the quantifier "all
Brazilians" with a variable.

00:28:31.370 --> 00:28:33.490 align:middle line:84%
So we start off with "All
Brazilians love soccer."

00:28:33.490 --> 00:28:35.115 align:middle line:84%
We're going to replace
"All Brazilians"

00:28:35.115 --> 00:28:36.940 align:middle line:90%
with a variable called x.

00:28:36.940 --> 00:28:38.110 align:middle line:90%
So x loves soccer.

00:28:38.110 --> 00:28:40.000 align:middle line:84%
So the second set
is the set of x.

00:28:40.000 --> 00:28:43.090 align:middle line:84%
We have this property
x loves soccer.

00:28:43.090 --> 00:28:46.385 align:middle line:84%
This is just an attempt to do
very slightly more formally

00:28:46.385 --> 00:28:48.010 align:middle line:84%
what I've been kind
of breezing through

00:28:48.010 --> 00:28:49.180 align:middle line:90%
in the preceding slides.

00:28:49.180 --> 00:28:52.630 align:middle line:84%
But does any of this seem
alarming or disturbing?

00:28:52.630 --> 00:28:54.430 align:middle line:90%
Is it causing unhappiness?

00:28:54.430 --> 00:28:56.972 align:middle line:84%
Have you all had your
nattoo this morning?

00:28:56.972 --> 00:28:58.930 align:middle line:84%
This is good-- maybe I
should bring some nattoo

00:28:58.930 --> 00:29:00.990 align:middle line:84%
and I can stand
around and watch.

00:29:00.990 --> 00:29:04.240 align:middle line:90%
It would be revenge.

00:29:04.240 --> 00:29:06.338 align:middle line:84%
I will not bring
a balut to class.

00:29:06.338 --> 00:29:07.630 align:middle line:90%
Not sure where I would get one.

00:29:07.630 --> 00:29:10.710 align:middle line:90%


00:29:10.710 --> 00:29:15.720 align:middle line:84%
So it's worth doing this because
doing this slight formalization

00:29:15.720 --> 00:29:18.030 align:middle line:84%
of what we've been doing
because, well, I've

00:29:18.030 --> 00:29:20.088 align:middle line:84%
have been carefully
giving you examples

00:29:20.088 --> 00:29:21.630 align:middle line:84%
in which the quantifier
is a subject,

00:29:21.630 --> 00:29:23.880 align:middle line:84%
but, of course, quantifiers
don't have to be subjects.

00:29:23.880 --> 00:29:28.260 align:middle line:84%
You can say things like
"Soccer bores all Americans."

00:29:28.260 --> 00:29:31.840 align:middle line:84%
Here, the first set is going
to be the set of Americans.

00:29:31.840 --> 00:29:34.100 align:middle line:90%
What's the second set?

00:29:34.100 --> 00:29:35.870 align:middle line:84%
So "Americans" as a
subset of what set?

00:29:35.870 --> 00:29:38.890 align:middle line:90%


00:29:38.890 --> 00:29:41.700 align:middle line:90%
Again, what we do is--

00:29:41.700 --> 00:29:42.780 align:middle line:90%
sorry, go ahead.

00:29:42.780 --> 00:29:44.657 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: X such
that soccer bores x?

00:29:44.657 --> 00:29:46.740 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah, x
such that soccer bores x.

00:29:46.740 --> 00:29:49.890 align:middle line:84%
So we take this sentence, we
replace "all Americans" with x,

00:29:49.890 --> 00:29:53.532 align:middle line:84%
and now we have the set of
x such that soccer bores x.

00:29:53.532 --> 00:29:55.240 align:middle line:84%
X doesn't have to be
in subject position.

00:29:55.240 --> 00:29:56.760 align:middle line:90%
That's the only point here.

00:29:56.760 --> 00:29:59.022 align:middle line:90%
That can be anywhere.

00:29:59.022 --> 00:30:03.090 align:middle line:84%
So that's how we'll do
the meaning of qualifiers.

00:30:03.090 --> 00:30:05.490 align:middle line:84%
So it says "Americans"
is a subset

00:30:05.490 --> 00:30:09.060 align:middle line:84%
of the people whom soccer bores,
the x such that soccer bores x.

00:30:09.060 --> 00:30:11.310 align:middle line:84%
So the quantifier has been
replaced with the variable.

00:30:11.310 --> 00:30:15.540 align:middle line:90%


00:30:15.540 --> 00:30:19.020 align:middle line:84%
So now we can get
back to the ambiguity

00:30:19.020 --> 00:30:21.900 align:middle line:84%
that I started us
off with, and I

00:30:21.900 --> 00:30:26.188 align:middle line:84%
will try to do this
slowly and carefully.

00:30:26.188 --> 00:30:28.230 align:middle line:84%
What we're going to see
is that a way of thinking

00:30:28.230 --> 00:30:30.660 align:middle line:84%
about the ambiguity that
I started this off with

00:30:30.660 --> 00:30:33.990 align:middle line:84%
is to think, yeah, you
have these two qualifiers

00:30:33.990 --> 00:30:36.060 align:middle line:84%
that are doing these
operations involving

00:30:36.060 --> 00:30:38.518 align:middle line:90%
the formation of sets.

00:30:38.518 --> 00:30:40.560 align:middle line:84%
So in all of the sentences
I've given you before,

00:30:40.560 --> 00:30:42.120 align:middle line:84%
there's only been
one quantifier,

00:30:42.120 --> 00:30:43.890 align:middle line:84%
so it forms these
sets and asserts

00:30:43.890 --> 00:30:45.170 align:middle line:90%
a relation between them.

00:30:45.170 --> 00:30:47.670 align:middle line:84%
But in a sentence like this,
where there are two qualifiers,

00:30:47.670 --> 00:30:50.850 align:middle line:84%
well, you're going to need to
perform that kind of operation

00:30:50.850 --> 00:30:53.070 align:middle line:90%
twice.

00:30:53.070 --> 00:30:55.770 align:middle line:84%
And the ambiguity just has to
do with the order in which you

00:30:55.770 --> 00:30:57.370 align:middle line:90%
perform the operations.

00:30:57.370 --> 00:30:59.640 align:middle line:84%
So do you do the
operations for "some child"

00:30:59.640 --> 00:31:02.670 align:middle line:84%
first and then the
operations for "every puppy,"

00:31:02.670 --> 00:31:04.740 align:middle line:84%
or do you do the operations
for "every puppy"

00:31:04.740 --> 00:31:08.230 align:middle line:84%
first and then the
operations for "some child"?

00:31:08.230 --> 00:31:11.220 align:middle line:84%
So if I remember my
own slides correctly,

00:31:11.220 --> 00:31:13.620 align:middle line:84%
I think what we're going to
do now is work fairly slowly

00:31:13.620 --> 00:31:15.787 align:middle line:84%
and painfully through what
happens if you do the two

00:31:15.787 --> 00:31:17.050 align:middle line:90%
things in that order.

00:31:17.050 --> 00:31:19.290 align:middle line:84%
But I'm telling you
this now in advance

00:31:19.290 --> 00:31:21.240 align:middle line:84%
to give you some
hope that you will

00:31:21.240 --> 00:31:24.450 align:middle line:84%
have an understanding by
the time we're done of what

00:31:24.450 --> 00:31:25.540 align:middle line:90%
the heck is going on here.

00:31:25.540 --> 00:31:29.490 align:middle line:84%
It's just what order do
you do the operations in.

00:31:29.490 --> 00:31:31.410 align:middle line:84%
That's why there's
ambiguity here.

00:31:31.410 --> 00:31:33.920 align:middle line:84%
You have these two
qualifiers and you

00:31:33.920 --> 00:31:35.920 align:middle line:84%
have options about which
one to interpret first.

00:31:35.920 --> 00:31:38.490 align:middle line:84%
So if you interpret
"every" first, then you're

00:31:38.490 --> 00:31:42.540 align:middle line:84%
saying the set of puppies is
a subset of the set of things

00:31:42.540 --> 00:31:43.620 align:middle line:90%
that some child loves.

00:31:43.620 --> 00:31:46.730 align:middle line:90%


00:31:46.730 --> 00:31:51.020 align:middle line:84%
And then, if you
interpret that part,

00:31:51.020 --> 00:31:54.590 align:middle line:84%
you're going to say,
what's the set of things

00:31:54.590 --> 00:31:55.640 align:middle line:90%
that some child loves?

00:31:55.640 --> 00:31:58.190 align:middle line:84%
Well, it's the set such
that the intersection

00:31:58.190 --> 00:32:00.680 align:middle line:84%
of the set of children
with the set of things

00:32:00.680 --> 00:32:03.525 align:middle line:90%
that loves them is nonempty.

00:32:03.525 --> 00:32:05.900 align:middle line:84%
So if we interpret "every"
first, then the sentence means

00:32:05.900 --> 00:32:08.750 align:middle line:84%
the set of puppies is
a subset of the set

00:32:08.750 --> 00:32:11.870 align:middle line:84%
of things such that
the intersection

00:32:11.870 --> 00:32:14.300 align:middle line:84%
of the set of children
with the set of things

00:32:14.300 --> 00:32:18.590 align:middle line:84%
that love those things
is nonempty-- whew.

00:32:18.590 --> 00:32:22.430 align:middle line:84%
If we do the operations in
that order we get that reading.

00:32:22.430 --> 00:32:26.120 align:middle line:84%
That is, we get the
reading the set of puppies

00:32:26.120 --> 00:32:29.150 align:middle line:84%
is a subset of the things
that some child loves.

00:32:29.150 --> 00:32:32.003 align:middle line:84%
Some child loves many
things, possibly,

00:32:32.003 --> 00:32:33.920 align:middle line:84%
and among those things
is the set of puppies--

00:32:33.920 --> 00:32:35.570 align:middle line:84%
so every member of
the set of puppies

00:32:35.570 --> 00:32:39.350 align:middle line:84%
is such that the
intersection of children such

00:32:39.350 --> 00:32:41.450 align:middle line:84%
that there is some
child that loves it.

00:32:41.450 --> 00:32:43.790 align:middle line:84%
And I think I have a picture
of that now-- yeah, there.

00:32:43.790 --> 00:32:47.473 align:middle line:84%
So doing the operations in that
order, we get this reading.

00:32:47.473 --> 00:32:48.890 align:middle line:84%
Every member of
the set of puppies

00:32:48.890 --> 00:32:52.640 align:middle line:84%
has this property-- there
is some child that loves it.

00:32:52.640 --> 00:32:54.590 align:middle line:84%
That's a reading the
sentence can have,

00:32:54.590 --> 00:32:58.160 align:middle line:84%
and we can get this reading
by doing the interpretation

00:32:58.160 --> 00:33:00.920 align:middle line:90%
of every puppy first.

00:33:00.920 --> 00:33:04.060 align:middle line:84%
That's one reading
the sentence can have.

00:33:04.060 --> 00:33:06.272 align:middle line:84%
So that's the reading
that's pictured here,

00:33:06.272 --> 00:33:08.230 align:middle line:84%
where there are many
puppies and many children,

00:33:08.230 --> 00:33:11.710 align:middle line:84%
and every puppy is loved by
at least one child, possibly

00:33:11.710 --> 00:33:13.130 align:middle line:90%
more than one.

00:33:13.130 --> 00:33:15.610 align:middle line:84%
There are also children who
love more than one puppy.

00:33:15.610 --> 00:33:18.220 align:middle line:84%
Children are not monogamous
when it comes to puppies.

00:33:18.220 --> 00:33:22.440 align:middle line:90%


00:33:22.440 --> 00:33:25.050 align:middle line:84%
What if you interpreted
"some child" first?

00:33:25.050 --> 00:33:27.810 align:middle line:84%
Well, then you'd be saying
take the set of children

00:33:27.810 --> 00:33:31.290 align:middle line:84%
and the set of things
that love every puppy.

00:33:31.290 --> 00:33:34.870 align:middle line:90%
That intersection is nonempty.

00:33:34.870 --> 00:33:36.490 align:middle line:84%
So take a deep
breath because we're

00:33:36.490 --> 00:33:37.840 align:middle line:84%
about to interpret the
second part of that,

00:33:37.840 --> 00:33:40.090 align:middle line:84%
but even before we interpret
the second part of that,

00:33:40.090 --> 00:33:41.715 align:middle line:84%
maybe this is the
point at which things

00:33:41.715 --> 00:33:43.720 align:middle line:84%
are as coherent as
they're ever going to get.

00:33:43.720 --> 00:33:47.620 align:middle line:84%
What this means is there
is at least one child

00:33:47.620 --> 00:33:49.390 align:middle line:90%
that loves every puppy.

00:33:49.390 --> 00:33:51.922 align:middle line:84%
There's at least one child
who's very fond of puppies.

00:33:51.922 --> 00:33:53.380 align:middle line:84%
That's the other
kind of reading we

00:33:53.380 --> 00:33:56.560 align:middle line:84%
were saying this kind
of sentence could have.

00:33:56.560 --> 00:33:58.570 align:middle line:90%
So we next interpret that.

00:33:58.570 --> 00:34:00.370 align:middle line:84%
So the intersection
of the set of children

00:34:00.370 --> 00:34:03.130 align:middle line:84%
and the set of things such
that the set of puppies

00:34:03.130 --> 00:34:07.542 align:middle line:84%
is a subset of the things that
that thing loves is nonempty.

00:34:07.542 --> 00:34:09.000 align:middle line:84%
And to put that
back in English, it

00:34:09.000 --> 00:34:11.370 align:middle line:84%
means there is at
least one child such

00:34:11.370 --> 00:34:13.469 align:middle line:84%
that the set of puppies
is a subset of the things

00:34:13.469 --> 00:34:14.472 align:middle line:90%
that x loves.

00:34:14.472 --> 00:34:16.889 align:middle line:84%
Or to put it another way, there
is at least one child such

00:34:16.889 --> 00:34:18.347 align:middle line:84%
that all puppies
are loved by them.

00:34:18.347 --> 00:34:20.820 align:middle line:90%


00:34:20.820 --> 00:34:25.080 align:middle line:84%
So early in this-- maybe one of
the first things I said today

00:34:25.080 --> 00:34:28.590 align:middle line:84%
was a sentence like "Some
child loves every puppy"--

00:34:28.590 --> 00:34:31.590 align:middle line:84%
or I think the example back then
was "Someone loves everyone"--

00:34:31.590 --> 00:34:33.330 align:middle line:84%
people have argued
that it's ambiguous,

00:34:33.330 --> 00:34:35.760 align:middle line:84%
that it can mean either
there is one person who

00:34:35.760 --> 00:34:40.398 align:middle line:84%
loves everyone or everyone
has someone that loves them.

00:34:40.398 --> 00:34:42.690 align:middle line:84%
And these are both meanings
that the sentence can have.

00:34:42.690 --> 00:34:44.491 align:middle line:84%
And I switched over to
people and languages

00:34:44.491 --> 00:34:46.949 align:middle line:84%
because our intuitions about
the difference between the two

00:34:46.949 --> 00:34:50.870 align:middle line:84%
meanings are a little sharper
for that kind of example.

00:34:50.870 --> 00:34:52.370 align:middle line:84%
Here, I'm showing
you some mechanics

00:34:52.370 --> 00:34:55.730 align:middle line:84%
that we can use to
get this ambiguity,

00:34:55.730 --> 00:35:01.100 align:middle line:84%
and the mechanics
involve allowing yourself

00:35:01.100 --> 00:35:03.638 align:middle line:84%
to do the operations
for interpreting

00:35:03.638 --> 00:35:05.930 align:middle line:84%
the quantifier-- when you
have more than one quantifier

00:35:05.930 --> 00:35:08.790 align:middle line:84%
in a sentence, you're allowed to
interpret them in either order.

00:35:08.790 --> 00:35:11.900 align:middle line:84%
So you're allowed to do the
operations for the subject

00:35:11.900 --> 00:35:13.793 align:middle line:84%
first or the operations
for the object first,

00:35:13.793 --> 00:35:15.710 align:middle line:84%
and depending on which
of those things you do,

00:35:15.710 --> 00:35:19.160 align:middle line:84%
you get these two different
readings for the sentence.

00:35:19.160 --> 00:35:21.980 align:middle line:90%
So far so good?

00:35:21.980 --> 00:35:25.700 align:middle line:84%
Sorry, this is a lot to deal
with on a Tuesday morning

00:35:25.700 --> 00:35:26.545 align:middle line:90%
through a mask.

00:35:26.545 --> 00:35:35.230 align:middle line:90%


00:35:35.230 --> 00:35:38.310 align:middle line:84%
So here's the tree
for that sentence

00:35:38.310 --> 00:35:40.050 align:middle line:90%
"some child loves every puppy."

00:35:40.050 --> 00:35:43.320 align:middle line:84%
And we've said that's
an ambiguous tree.

00:35:43.320 --> 00:35:46.320 align:middle line:90%
So why is the tree ambiguous?

00:35:46.320 --> 00:35:48.790 align:middle line:84%
What I've now said a couple
of times-- and who knows,

00:35:48.790 --> 00:35:50.010 align:middle line:90%
this could be true--

00:35:50.010 --> 00:35:53.100 align:middle line:84%
is yeah, you've got
these two qualifiers.

00:35:53.100 --> 00:35:58.507 align:middle line:84%
You've got to perform
operations that form these sets.

00:35:58.507 --> 00:36:00.840 align:middle line:84%
The part of the process of
interpreting these qualifiers

00:36:00.840 --> 00:36:04.440 align:middle line:84%
involves forming these sets and
there's nothing in particular

00:36:04.440 --> 00:36:07.290 align:middle line:84%
telling you what order to
perform those operations in.

00:36:07.290 --> 00:36:10.440 align:middle line:84%
And so you can interpret
them in either order,

00:36:10.440 --> 00:36:12.120 align:middle line:90%
and so you get ambiguities.

00:36:12.120 --> 00:36:13.920 align:middle line:84%
That's the way
I've been talking.

00:36:13.920 --> 00:36:17.410 align:middle line:84%
That turns out to be a lie,
which is kind of interesting.

00:36:17.410 --> 00:36:22.550 align:middle line:84%
We have lots of good evidence,
and I'll show you some of it,

00:36:22.550 --> 00:36:25.580 align:middle line:84%
that we don't just get to freely
choose the order in which we

00:36:25.580 --> 00:36:27.020 align:middle line:90%
interpret qualifiers.

00:36:27.020 --> 00:36:31.640 align:middle line:84%
But what really happens is
that there is another kind

00:36:31.640 --> 00:36:33.600 align:middle line:90%
of movement operation.

00:36:33.600 --> 00:36:36.860 align:middle line:84%
So we've left syntax behind,
and maybe some of you

00:36:36.860 --> 00:36:40.190 align:middle line:84%
were hoping we wouldn't have
to see things move anymore,

00:36:40.190 --> 00:36:43.130 align:middle line:84%
but actually, in semantics,
there's also movement going on,

00:36:43.130 --> 00:36:46.588 align:middle line:84%
possibly movement of an even
sneakier and weirder kind.

00:36:46.588 --> 00:36:49.130 align:middle line:84%
There's lots of evidence that
the reason that the sentence is

00:36:49.130 --> 00:36:52.070 align:middle line:84%
ambiguous is that there's
an operation which

00:36:52.070 --> 00:36:55.490 align:middle line:84%
is optional that takes
the object "every puppy"

00:36:55.490 --> 00:37:01.420 align:middle line:84%
and moves it to a position above
"some child" in the process

00:37:01.420 --> 00:37:04.280 align:middle line:90%
of interpreting the sentence.

00:37:04.280 --> 00:37:07.670 align:middle line:84%
Now there are several alarming
things about this movement.

00:37:07.670 --> 00:37:10.730 align:middle line:84%
All of the movements that
I've shown you up until now--

00:37:10.730 --> 00:37:13.040 align:middle line:84%
so when I first tried
introducing you to movement,

00:37:13.040 --> 00:37:21.110 align:middle line:84%
I think I was showing you things
like "Mary devoured a pizza."

00:37:21.110 --> 00:37:23.060 align:middle line:84%
I was saying yeah,
look, the pizza

00:37:23.060 --> 00:37:24.500 align:middle line:90%
absolutely has to be here.

00:37:24.500 --> 00:37:27.800 align:middle line:90%
You can't say "Mary devoured."

00:37:27.800 --> 00:37:30.290 align:middle line:90%
That's out.

00:37:30.290 --> 00:37:34.820 align:middle line:84%
So "devour" selects for
a sister-- it absolutely

00:37:34.820 --> 00:37:35.870 align:middle line:90%
has to have a sister.

00:37:35.870 --> 00:37:37.515 align:middle line:84%
And moreover, its
sister absolutely

00:37:37.515 --> 00:37:38.640 align:middle line:90%
has to be right next to it.

00:37:38.640 --> 00:37:42.870 align:middle line:84%
So if I put an adverb
here, it gets bad.

00:37:42.870 --> 00:37:45.513 align:middle line:84%
So Mary devoured quickly
a pizza-- that's no good.

00:37:45.513 --> 00:37:47.930 align:middle line:84%
So we know something about the
properties of the "devour."

00:37:47.930 --> 00:37:49.740 align:middle line:84%
It absolutely has
to have an object,

00:37:49.740 --> 00:37:51.740 align:middle line:84%
and the object absolutely
has to be right after.

00:37:51.740 --> 00:37:53.250 align:middle line:90%
It cannot be anywhere else.

00:37:53.250 --> 00:37:55.625 align:middle line:84%
And then I said, wait, what
about "what did Mary devour?"

00:37:55.625 --> 00:37:58.750 align:middle line:90%


00:37:58.750 --> 00:38:05.830 align:middle line:84%
And we ended up deciding that
"what" is starting off here

00:38:05.830 --> 00:38:08.080 align:middle line:90%
and it's moving up to here.

00:38:08.080 --> 00:38:09.830 align:middle line:90%
And that's why it's over there.

00:38:09.830 --> 00:38:13.690 align:middle line:84%
So we said the process of
producing a sentence like "what

00:38:13.690 --> 00:38:16.300 align:middle line:84%
did Mary devour" involves
"what" starting off

00:38:16.300 --> 00:38:17.530 align:middle line:90%
exactly where it should--

00:38:17.530 --> 00:38:20.590 align:middle line:84%
as the sister of "devour"
right next to "devour"--

00:38:20.590 --> 00:38:22.180 align:middle line:84%
and then there's
this other process

00:38:22.180 --> 00:38:24.130 align:middle line:84%
that doesn't care about
what "devour" wants.

00:38:24.130 --> 00:38:25.572 align:middle line:84%
This process of
wh- movement that

00:38:25.572 --> 00:38:28.030 align:middle line:84%
takes "what" and moves it to
the beginning of the sentence,

00:38:28.030 --> 00:38:28.530 align:middle line:90%
we saw.

00:38:28.530 --> 00:38:31.210 align:middle line:84%
Some languages have
this and others don't.

00:38:31.210 --> 00:38:32.710 align:middle line:84%
I was looking at
WALS the other day.

00:38:32.710 --> 00:38:34.918 align:middle line:84%
It turns out roughly a third
of the world's languages

00:38:34.918 --> 00:38:36.338 align:middle line:90%
have this, and English does.

00:38:36.338 --> 00:38:38.380 align:middle line:84%
So you take what and you
move it to the beginning

00:38:38.380 --> 00:38:40.960 align:middle line:90%
of the sentence.

00:38:40.960 --> 00:38:43.210 align:middle line:84%
So every kind of movement
that we've seen up until now

00:38:43.210 --> 00:38:44.170 align:middle line:90%
has been like this.

00:38:44.170 --> 00:38:46.060 align:middle line:84%
It's been part of an
explanation for why

00:38:46.060 --> 00:38:48.220 align:middle line:84%
you pronounce things
in different places

00:38:48.220 --> 00:38:50.088 align:middle line:90%
than you would expect.

00:38:50.088 --> 00:38:51.880 align:middle line:84%
You would expect "devour"
to have a sister,

00:38:51.880 --> 00:38:53.838 align:middle line:84%
to have an object that
would be right after it.

00:38:53.838 --> 00:38:56.350 align:middle line:84%
And "what" isn't there, and
it's because of a movement

00:38:56.350 --> 00:38:58.122 align:middle line:90%
operation.

00:38:58.122 --> 00:38:59.580 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to give
you some evidence

00:38:59.580 --> 00:39:02.100 align:middle line:84%
now that this ambiguity
shows up because you have

00:39:02.100 --> 00:39:04.920 align:middle line:84%
the option of moving
"every puppy" to a position

00:39:04.920 --> 00:39:06.570 align:middle line:84%
to the left of
"some child," higher

00:39:06.570 --> 00:39:08.910 align:middle line:90%
than "some child" in the tree.

00:39:08.910 --> 00:39:11.640 align:middle line:84%
But this had better be a
different kind of movement

00:39:11.640 --> 00:39:13.890 align:middle line:84%
because the sentence
is ambiguous

00:39:13.890 --> 00:39:18.970 align:middle line:84%
even if you don't see the noun
phrases going anywhere else.

00:39:18.970 --> 00:39:25.800 align:middle line:84%
This is a type of movement
called covert movement,

00:39:25.800 --> 00:39:29.100 align:middle line:84%
and you should all be
very suspicious of this

00:39:29.100 --> 00:39:34.200 align:middle line:84%
because basically,
it's like the--

00:39:34.200 --> 00:39:35.070 align:middle line:90%
what's it like?

00:39:35.070 --> 00:39:37.200 align:middle line:84%
It's like the dark
matter that physicists

00:39:37.200 --> 00:39:38.700 align:middle line:84%
have to posit in
order to explain

00:39:38.700 --> 00:39:41.130 align:middle line:84%
why most of the
matter in the universe

00:39:41.130 --> 00:39:42.660 align:middle line:90%
doesn't seem to be there.

00:39:42.660 --> 00:39:45.540 align:middle line:84%
So there's all this undetectable
matter that's out there.

00:39:45.540 --> 00:39:47.553 align:middle line:90%
We just haven't detected it yet.

00:39:47.553 --> 00:39:49.470 align:middle line:84%
Somehow, when physicists
say things like this,

00:39:49.470 --> 00:39:52.390 align:middle line:84%
we believe them because they
can build bombs and things.

00:39:52.390 --> 00:39:55.190 align:middle line:84%
But I'm about to say something
kind of similar to that.

00:39:55.190 --> 00:39:56.940 align:middle line:84%
We have some arguments,
really good ones--

00:39:56.940 --> 00:39:58.920 align:middle line:84%
I'll try to show you
some of them, anyway--

00:39:58.920 --> 00:40:01.140 align:middle line:90%
that this thing can move.

00:40:01.140 --> 00:40:04.290 align:middle line:84%
And the fact that when it
moves, you still pronounce it

00:40:04.290 --> 00:40:07.710 align:middle line:84%
as though it hadn't moved,
this is really interesting,

00:40:07.710 --> 00:40:10.990 align:middle line:84%
but what it shows is
that this is dark matter.

00:40:10.990 --> 00:40:11.700 align:middle line:90%
It is moving.

00:40:11.700 --> 00:40:13.117 align:middle line:84%
It's just not
moving in a way that

00:40:13.117 --> 00:40:16.778 align:middle line:84%
changes the order of the
sentence, order of the words.

00:40:16.778 --> 00:40:18.570 align:middle line:84%
All of you are making
the appropriate face,

00:40:18.570 --> 00:40:19.770 align:middle line:90%
which is a very skeptical one.

00:40:19.770 --> 00:40:21.160 align:middle line:84%
I mean, some of you
are wearing masks,

00:40:21.160 --> 00:40:23.285 align:middle line:84%
so I can only see your
skeptical eyebrows and eyes,

00:40:23.285 --> 00:40:26.098 align:middle line:90%
but I appreciate that.

00:40:26.098 --> 00:40:27.390 align:middle line:90%
Let me show you some arguments.

00:40:27.390 --> 00:40:32.210 align:middle line:84%
First, there are
some languages--

00:40:32.210 --> 00:40:34.410 align:middle line:84%
yeah, so here's another
ambiguous example.

00:40:34.410 --> 00:40:37.640 align:middle line:90%
"Most people ate two cakes."

00:40:37.640 --> 00:40:40.160 align:middle line:90%
That can mean two things.

00:40:40.160 --> 00:40:42.500 align:middle line:84%
It can mean either-- what's
one thing it can mean?

00:40:42.500 --> 00:40:47.370 align:middle line:90%


00:40:47.370 --> 00:40:49.770 align:middle line:84%
Softening you up for
the next set of slides.

00:40:49.770 --> 00:40:52.200 align:middle line:84%
You don't have to use
x's and y's and things.

00:40:52.200 --> 00:40:54.090 align:middle line:84%
Just tell me what
it seems to mean.

00:40:54.090 --> 00:40:57.270 align:middle line:90%


00:40:57.270 --> 00:40:57.770 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:40:57.770 --> 00:40:59.102 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Most of the
people in the room

00:40:59.102 --> 00:41:00.620 align:middle line:90%
ate two cakes for each person.

00:41:00.620 --> 00:41:01.320 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
For each person.

00:41:01.320 --> 00:41:03.080 align:middle line:84%
So there were a
whole bunch of cakes,

00:41:03.080 --> 00:41:04.692 align:middle line:90%
and there was a free for all.

00:41:04.692 --> 00:41:06.650 align:middle line:84%
People could have as many
cakes as they wanted,

00:41:06.650 --> 00:41:09.067 align:middle line:84%
and we were keeping track of
how many cakes people wanted,

00:41:09.067 --> 00:41:10.550 align:middle line:90%
and most people ate two cakes.

00:41:10.550 --> 00:41:12.170 align:middle line:90%
Some people ate three.

00:41:12.170 --> 00:41:15.440 align:middle line:84%
There was this Richards
guy who ate 12.

00:41:15.440 --> 00:41:17.180 align:middle line:84%
And then some people
didn't eat any.

00:41:17.180 --> 00:41:18.290 align:middle line:90%
But most people ate two.

00:41:18.290 --> 00:41:19.940 align:middle line:90%
That's one thing it can mean.

00:41:19.940 --> 00:41:22.110 align:middle line:84%
What's another
thing it can mean?

00:41:22.110 --> 00:41:22.865 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:41:22.865 --> 00:41:24.990 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: There are a total
of two cakes from which

00:41:24.990 --> 00:41:26.100 align:middle line:90%
most people have eaten.

00:41:26.100 --> 00:41:28.950 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah, so
there were various cakes

00:41:28.950 --> 00:41:30.600 align:middle line:84%
out there, maybe
really big cakes

00:41:30.600 --> 00:41:33.360 align:middle line:84%
that you could eat a slice
of, and we were finding out

00:41:33.360 --> 00:41:34.830 align:middle line:84%
what the most
popular cakes were,

00:41:34.830 --> 00:41:38.220 align:middle line:84%
and most people ate
two cakes, the vanilla

00:41:38.220 --> 00:41:40.110 align:middle line:84%
frosted and the
chocolate frosted,

00:41:40.110 --> 00:41:41.918 align:middle line:84%
and then there were a
couple of other cakes

00:41:41.918 --> 00:41:42.960 align:middle line:90%
that were not as popular.

00:41:42.960 --> 00:41:44.250 align:middle line:90%
So concentrate on those.

00:41:44.250 --> 00:41:46.050 align:middle line:90%
It can kind of mean that.

00:41:46.050 --> 00:41:48.780 align:middle line:84%
Some of you were raising
your eyebrows at that.

00:41:48.780 --> 00:41:51.360 align:middle line:90%
But it can sort of mean that.

00:41:51.360 --> 00:41:53.460 align:middle line:84%
So one reason to take
seriously the idea

00:41:53.460 --> 00:41:55.890 align:middle line:84%
that this ambiguity comes
from an optional movement

00:41:55.890 --> 00:42:00.270 align:middle line:84%
operation which you can't see
is that there are languages

00:42:00.270 --> 00:42:02.410 align:middle line:90%
in which you can see it.

00:42:02.410 --> 00:42:05.290 align:middle line:84%
So you remember how I said
English has wh- movement

00:42:05.290 --> 00:42:08.230 align:middle line:84%
and there are languages out
there that don't, languages

00:42:08.230 --> 00:42:12.010 align:middle line:84%
like Mandarin or Japanese
or Chaha or whatever?

00:42:12.010 --> 00:42:14.440 align:middle line:84%
Similarly, this
operation that takes one

00:42:14.440 --> 00:42:17.140 align:middle line:84%
quantifier and moves it
past another quantifier--

00:42:17.140 --> 00:42:18.640 align:middle line:84%
you don't get to
see it in English,

00:42:18.640 --> 00:42:21.610 align:middle line:84%
but you do get to see it,
for example, in Hungarian.

00:42:21.610 --> 00:42:27.350 align:middle line:84%
So in Hungarian, the Hungarian
translations for "Most people

00:42:27.350 --> 00:42:34.030 align:middle line:84%
ate two cakes" have two
word orders, each of which

00:42:34.030 --> 00:42:36.400 align:middle line:90%
means only a single thing.

00:42:36.400 --> 00:42:38.242 align:middle line:84%
So you either say,
in Hungarian--

00:42:38.242 --> 00:42:39.700 align:middle line:84%
I don't speak
Hungarian and I won't

00:42:39.700 --> 00:42:40.825 align:middle line:90%
try to say these sentences.

00:42:40.825 --> 00:42:42.980 align:middle line:84%
Does anyone here
speak Hungarian?

00:42:42.980 --> 00:42:45.055 align:middle line:90%
OK, that's very freeing.

00:42:45.055 --> 00:42:47.555 align:middle line:84%
I almost feel as though I should
try to say these sentences.

00:42:47.555 --> 00:42:49.930 align:middle line:84%
I know you don't know what
Hungarian is supposed to sound

00:42:49.930 --> 00:42:52.530 align:middle line:90%
like, but I won't.

00:42:52.530 --> 00:42:55.700 align:middle line:84%
The first sentence
only has one meaning,

00:42:55.700 --> 00:42:58.200 align:middle line:84%
and the second sentence
also has only one meaning.

00:42:58.200 --> 00:43:00.320 align:middle line:84%
So you can take these
expressions "most people"

00:43:00.320 --> 00:43:02.960 align:middle line:84%
and "from two cakes" and
put them in either order,

00:43:02.960 --> 00:43:05.540 align:middle line:84%
and depending on
what order you're in,

00:43:05.540 --> 00:43:06.780 align:middle line:90%
you only get one reading.

00:43:06.780 --> 00:43:11.720 align:middle line:84%
So the first sentence means the
first reading that was offered,

00:43:11.720 --> 00:43:16.520 align:middle line:84%
the most reasonable
reading, most of the people

00:43:16.520 --> 00:43:18.590 align:middle line:90%
ate two kinds of cake.

00:43:18.590 --> 00:43:22.160 align:middle line:84%
The second meaning means there
were two particular cakes that

00:43:22.160 --> 00:43:24.480 align:middle line:90%
were the most popular ones.

00:43:24.480 --> 00:43:26.930 align:middle line:84%
So in Hungarian,
the order in which

00:43:26.930 --> 00:43:29.480 align:middle line:84%
you apply the operations
to interpret the qualifiers

00:43:29.480 --> 00:43:31.050 align:middle line:90%
is not optional.

00:43:31.050 --> 00:43:32.090 align:middle line:90%
It's completely fixed.

00:43:32.090 --> 00:43:34.785 align:middle line:84%
It's determined by the
order of the phrases.

00:43:34.785 --> 00:43:36.410 align:middle line:84%
That's what determines
what's going on.

00:43:36.410 --> 00:43:38.840 align:middle line:84%
And the claim is
that all languages

00:43:38.840 --> 00:43:42.560 align:middle line:90%
are Hungarian deep down.

00:43:42.560 --> 00:43:47.040 align:middle line:84%
It's just that some languages
are better at this than others.

00:43:47.040 --> 00:43:49.820 align:middle line:84%
So there are languages
like Hungarian,

00:43:49.820 --> 00:43:52.250 align:middle line:84%
for example, that are really
good at being Hungarian.

00:43:52.250 --> 00:43:53.850 align:middle line:90%
You can just see it right there.

00:43:53.850 --> 00:43:55.308 align:middle line:84%
And then there are
other languages,

00:43:55.308 --> 00:43:58.410 align:middle line:84%
like English, that are Hungarian
but they're shy about it.

00:43:58.410 --> 00:44:01.070 align:middle line:84%
And so you don't get to see
the relevant kinds of movement.

00:44:01.070 --> 00:44:06.200 align:middle line:90%


00:44:06.200 --> 00:44:08.930 align:middle line:90%
OK, what are we doing?

00:44:08.930 --> 00:44:13.760 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to show
you some more reasons

00:44:13.760 --> 00:44:19.400 align:middle line:84%
to take this idea seriously that
this operation, which is called

00:44:19.400 --> 00:44:26.778 align:middle line:84%
quantifier raising,
or QR, that it exists.

00:44:26.778 --> 00:44:28.820 align:middle line:84%
So we'll talk about some
of the properties of it.

00:44:28.820 --> 00:44:33.045 align:middle line:90%


00:44:33.045 --> 00:44:34.920 align:middle line:84%
We'll talk about some
of the properties of it

00:44:34.920 --> 00:44:37.080 align:middle line:90%
in the next little while.

00:44:37.080 --> 00:44:39.630 align:middle line:84%
Here's another sentence
with two qualifiers in it.

00:44:39.630 --> 00:44:43.410 align:middle line:84%
This is work that I'm taking
from my colleague Danny

00:44:43.410 --> 00:44:45.270 align:middle line:84%
Fox, who's done a
lot of fantastic work

00:44:45.270 --> 00:44:48.330 align:middle line:84%
on the properties
of quantifiers.

00:44:48.330 --> 00:44:50.310 align:middle line:84%
So here's an ambiguous
sentence, "A guard

00:44:50.310 --> 00:44:52.530 align:middle line:84%
is standing in front
of every building."

00:44:52.530 --> 00:44:56.130 align:middle line:90%
What does this sentence mean?

00:44:56.130 --> 00:44:57.120 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:44:57.120 --> 00:44:59.850 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Either that every
building has a different guard

00:44:59.850 --> 00:45:01.225 align:middle line:84%
standing in front
of it, or there

00:45:01.225 --> 00:45:03.000 align:middle line:84%
is one guard that is
continuously standing

00:45:03.000 --> 00:45:03.810 align:middle line:90%
in front of every building.

00:45:03.810 --> 00:45:04.660 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Yeah, there we go.

00:45:04.660 --> 00:45:07.350 align:middle line:84%
So it has a sensible reading--
every building is guarded.

00:45:07.350 --> 00:45:12.210 align:middle line:84%
And it has a reading
involving a very large guard,

00:45:12.210 --> 00:45:15.480 align:middle line:84%
or maybe lots of really
small buildings or something

00:45:15.480 --> 00:45:17.955 align:middle line:90%
like that.

00:45:17.955 --> 00:45:20.580 align:middle line:84%
Oh yeah, no, let's imagine that
there's more than one building.

00:45:20.580 --> 00:45:23.410 align:middle line:90%


00:45:23.410 --> 00:45:25.410 align:middle line:84%
So those are two things
the sentence could mean.

00:45:25.410 --> 00:45:28.590 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, here are a bunch of
buildings, a bunch of guards,

00:45:28.590 --> 00:45:32.900 align:middle line:84%
one guard per building, or
a bunch of buildings and one

00:45:32.900 --> 00:45:35.090 align:middle line:90%
guard who is kind of wide.

00:45:35.090 --> 00:45:37.460 align:middle line:84%
So it could mean either
of those things that's it.

00:45:37.460 --> 00:45:40.300 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:45:40.300 --> 00:45:42.500 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Ah, yes,
so if the buildings are

00:45:42.500 --> 00:45:44.000 align:middle line:84%
all in a circle and they're
all facing each other,

00:45:44.000 --> 00:45:45.890 align:middle line:84%
there could be one
guard in the middle.

00:45:45.890 --> 00:45:48.050 align:middle line:84%
A guard is standing--
he managed to stand

00:45:48.050 --> 00:45:50.720 align:middle line:84%
in front of every
building by standing right

00:45:50.720 --> 00:45:51.673 align:middle line:90%
in the center spot.

00:45:51.673 --> 00:45:53.090 align:middle line:84%
Yes, I suppose it
could mean that.

00:45:53.090 --> 00:45:55.010 align:middle line:90%
I think that's right.

00:45:55.010 --> 00:45:58.820 align:middle line:90%
Let's discount that reading.

00:45:58.820 --> 00:46:01.228 align:middle line:84%
So here's an ambiguity,
the kind of ambiguity

00:46:01.228 --> 00:46:02.270 align:middle line:90%
we've been talking about.

00:46:02.270 --> 00:46:05.330 align:middle line:84%
You've got these two
qualifiers and you

00:46:05.330 --> 00:46:08.240 align:middle line:84%
can interpret-- so
the first pass on why

00:46:08.240 --> 00:46:10.190 align:middle line:84%
we got this kind of
ambiguity was there's

00:46:10.190 --> 00:46:14.480 align:middle line:84%
this process of interpreting
qualifiers where you figure out

00:46:14.480 --> 00:46:16.670 align:middle line:84%
what the sets are that the
quantifier is relating,

00:46:16.670 --> 00:46:18.795 align:middle line:84%
and you relate
them to each other.

00:46:18.795 --> 00:46:21.170 align:middle line:84%
And if you have a sentence
that has two qualifiers in it,

00:46:21.170 --> 00:46:22.970 align:middle line:90%
you get to do that process--

00:46:22.970 --> 00:46:25.250 align:middle line:84%
either you've got to quantifier,
quantifier number one

00:46:25.250 --> 00:46:26.420 align:middle line:90%
and quantifier number two.

00:46:26.420 --> 00:46:29.360 align:middle line:84%
You either apply that process
to quantifier number one first

00:46:29.360 --> 00:46:31.760 align:middle line:90%
or quantifier number two first.

00:46:31.760 --> 00:46:35.930 align:middle line:84%
And then I asserted and
offered you, so far,

00:46:35.930 --> 00:46:38.810 align:middle line:90%
only evidence from Hungarian.

00:46:38.810 --> 00:46:40.310 align:middle line:84%
I asserted that
there is actually

00:46:40.310 --> 00:46:42.590 align:middle line:84%
this operation of
quantifier raising

00:46:42.590 --> 00:46:46.550 align:middle line:84%
that has the option of moving
one quantifier to a higher

00:46:46.550 --> 00:46:48.170 align:middle line:84%
position than the
other quantifier,

00:46:48.170 --> 00:46:52.190 align:middle line:84%
and that the order in which
you perform the operations

00:46:52.190 --> 00:46:53.060 align:middle line:90%
is actually fixed.

00:46:53.060 --> 00:46:55.477 align:middle line:84%
It's completely determined
by the structure

00:46:55.477 --> 00:46:57.560 align:middle line:84%
that you get after you've
done quantifier raising,

00:46:57.560 --> 00:46:59.060 align:middle line:90%
if you have.

00:46:59.060 --> 00:47:01.070 align:middle line:84%
So what I'm going to do
in the next few slides

00:47:01.070 --> 00:47:04.580 align:middle line:84%
is show you some reasons to
take that seriously, even

00:47:04.580 --> 00:47:06.650 align:middle line:90%
for English.

00:47:06.650 --> 00:47:08.840 align:middle line:84%
So here's a sentence
that has ambiguity

00:47:08.840 --> 00:47:12.110 align:middle line:84%
and the story was going to be
it's ambiguous because you have

00:47:12.110 --> 00:47:15.200 align:middle line:84%
the option of leaving
"every building" where it is

00:47:15.200 --> 00:47:17.810 align:middle line:84%
or the option of moving
"every building" so that it's

00:47:17.810 --> 00:47:19.760 align:middle line:90%
higher than "a guard."

00:47:19.760 --> 00:47:24.510 align:middle line:84%
And if you take the option
of moving "every building"

00:47:24.510 --> 00:47:27.740 align:middle line:84%
so that it's higher than
"a guard," invisibly,

00:47:27.740 --> 00:47:29.390 align:middle line:84%
without changing
the word order--

00:47:29.390 --> 00:47:32.420 align:middle line:84%
very weird to try to
understand what's going on--

00:47:32.420 --> 00:47:35.630 align:middle line:84%
then what you do so
the generalization is

00:47:35.630 --> 00:47:38.670 align:middle line:84%
that you must interpret the
highest quantifier first.

00:47:38.670 --> 00:47:40.280 align:middle line:84%
So if you do QR of
"every building"

00:47:40.280 --> 00:47:43.130 align:middle line:84%
higher than "a guard," what
you're saying is you're

00:47:43.130 --> 00:47:46.100 align:middle line:84%
going to start by relating
the set of buildings

00:47:46.100 --> 00:47:49.310 align:middle line:84%
to the set of things that a
guard is standing in front of.

00:47:49.310 --> 00:47:51.590 align:middle line:84%
And you're going to say
that first set is completely

00:47:51.590 --> 00:47:53.660 align:middle line:90%
contained in the second set.

00:47:53.660 --> 00:47:57.860 align:middle line:84%
And then you will interpret
the quantifier of "guard."

00:47:57.860 --> 00:48:01.730 align:middle line:84%
So you have this
optional operation of QR,

00:48:01.730 --> 00:48:08.252 align:middle line:84%
and there isn't actually
any optionality in the order

00:48:08.252 --> 00:48:09.710 align:middle line:84%
in which you
interpret quantifiers.

00:48:09.710 --> 00:48:12.110 align:middle line:84%
You always interpret the
highest quantifier first.

00:48:12.110 --> 00:48:13.670 align:middle line:90%
That's going to be the story.

00:48:13.670 --> 00:48:15.590 align:middle line:84%
The optionality has to
do with the optionality

00:48:15.590 --> 00:48:19.042 align:middle line:90%
of the application of QR.

00:48:19.042 --> 00:48:21.000 align:middle line:84%
So here's a sentence with
two qualifiers in it,

00:48:21.000 --> 00:48:21.840 align:middle line:90%
and it's ambiguous.

00:48:21.840 --> 00:48:24.390 align:middle line:84%
And I'm asserting it's
ambiguous because you can do QR.

00:48:24.390 --> 00:48:26.610 align:middle line:84%
Here's another sentence
with two quantifiers in it:

00:48:26.610 --> 00:48:27.990 align:middle line:84%
"A guard said that
I should stand

00:48:27.990 --> 00:48:31.380 align:middle line:90%
in front of every building."

00:48:31.380 --> 00:48:34.250 align:middle line:84%
Now is this ambiguous
in the same way?

00:48:34.250 --> 00:48:34.750 align:middle line:90%
No.

00:48:34.750 --> 00:48:37.400 align:middle line:90%


00:48:37.400 --> 00:48:41.080 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't mean-- so
what does it mean?

00:48:41.080 --> 00:48:43.380 align:middle line:90%


00:48:43.380 --> 00:48:43.880 align:middle line:90%
Joseph?

00:48:43.880 --> 00:48:47.812 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The guard thinks that
I have to be particularly--

00:48:47.812 --> 00:48:48.770 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Large.

00:48:48.770 --> 00:48:51.690 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: --to be able to stand
in front of all [INAUDIBLE]..

00:48:51.690 --> 00:48:52.982 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah, exactly.

00:48:52.982 --> 00:48:57.090 align:middle line:84%
There is one guard who
demands that I gain weight--

00:48:57.090 --> 00:48:58.605 align:middle line:84%
wants me to be wide
enough to stand

00:48:58.605 --> 00:48:59.730 align:middle line:90%
in front of every building.

00:48:59.730 --> 00:49:00.230 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, Faith?

00:49:00.230 --> 00:49:04.500 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I interpreted it
instead as move from one

00:49:04.500 --> 00:49:06.180 align:middle line:90%
building to the next one.

00:49:06.180 --> 00:49:08.460 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah, that's
a more reasonable thing

00:49:08.460 --> 00:49:10.860 align:middle line:84%
for the guard to ask
me to do, isn't it?

00:49:10.860 --> 00:49:14.040 align:middle line:84%
And I guess it helps
that we've switched here

00:49:14.040 --> 00:49:18.510 align:middle line:84%
to "That I should stand in
front of every building"

00:49:18.510 --> 00:49:19.650 align:middle line:90%
rather than--

00:49:19.650 --> 00:49:21.150 align:middle line:84%
so what we had
before was a guard

00:49:21.150 --> 00:49:23.340 align:middle line:84%
is standing in front
of every building.

00:49:23.340 --> 00:49:25.020 align:middle line:84%
I guess we could interpret
that as meaning that there's

00:49:25.020 --> 00:49:27.210 align:middle line:84%
a single guard who is walking
from building to building

00:49:27.210 --> 00:49:28.500 align:middle line:84%
and standing briefly
in front of them.

00:49:28.500 --> 00:49:30.270 align:middle line:84%
These are all ways of
getting a single guard

00:49:30.270 --> 00:49:31.110 align:middle line:90%
to do all the work.

00:49:31.110 --> 00:49:32.068 align:middle line:90%
There's only one guard.

00:49:32.068 --> 00:49:35.297 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, you're absolutely right.

00:49:35.297 --> 00:49:36.880 align:middle line:84%
But it doesn't have
the other reading.

00:49:36.880 --> 00:49:40.112 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't mean there
was one guard who

00:49:40.112 --> 00:49:42.070 align:middle line:84%
wanted me to stand in
front of the Stata Center

00:49:42.070 --> 00:49:43.600 align:middle line:84%
and another guard who
wanted me to stand in front

00:49:43.600 --> 00:49:45.433 align:middle line:84%
of the Student Center,
and another guard who

00:49:45.433 --> 00:49:47.410 align:middle line:84%
wanted me to stand in
front of the MIT Museum.

00:49:47.410 --> 00:49:48.285 align:middle line:90%
It doesn't mean that.

00:49:48.285 --> 00:49:50.910 align:middle line:90%


00:49:50.910 --> 00:49:52.260 align:middle line:90%
All right, why not?

00:49:52.260 --> 00:49:55.290 align:middle line:84%
Because we've got
two qualifiers.

00:49:55.290 --> 00:49:57.510 align:middle line:84%
Why can't we interpret
these qualifiers

00:49:57.510 --> 00:49:59.582 align:middle line:90%
in any order we want?

00:49:59.582 --> 00:50:02.070 align:middle line:84%
Well, if we believe in QR,
we have an answer to that.

00:50:02.070 --> 00:50:04.340 align:middle line:84%
We get to say what's
special about this example

00:50:04.340 --> 00:50:05.930 align:middle line:84%
and distinguishes
it from the examples

00:50:05.930 --> 00:50:10.070 align:middle line:84%
we had before is that "a
guard" is in the matrix clause,

00:50:10.070 --> 00:50:12.650 align:middle line:84%
and there's an embedded
clause, an embedded CP,

00:50:12.650 --> 00:50:14.900 align:middle line:84%
that I should stand in
front of every building.

00:50:14.900 --> 00:50:17.150 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that's an embedded clause.

00:50:17.150 --> 00:50:17.900 align:middle line:90%
Is that clear?

00:50:17.900 --> 00:50:19.670 align:middle line:90%
Should I draw a tree?

00:50:19.670 --> 00:50:22.820 align:middle line:84%
So say it's taking,
as its sister,

00:50:22.820 --> 00:50:25.220 align:middle line:84%
a CP that I should stand
in front of every building.

00:50:25.220 --> 00:50:27.137 align:middle line:84%
And what we're learning
is that although there

00:50:27.137 --> 00:50:28.820 align:middle line:84%
is this optional
operation, QR, that

00:50:28.820 --> 00:50:30.680 align:middle line:84%
can get one quantifier
passed to another,

00:50:30.680 --> 00:50:33.215 align:middle line:84%
it can't get you
out of a clause.

00:50:33.215 --> 00:50:35.660 align:middle line:84%
We're going to have to figure
out what counts as a clause,

00:50:35.660 --> 00:50:37.550 align:middle line:84%
but apparently this
counts as a clause.

00:50:37.550 --> 00:50:40.670 align:middle line:84%
There's some limitation
on how far you can QR.

00:50:40.670 --> 00:50:44.920 align:middle line:90%


00:50:44.920 --> 00:50:45.790 align:middle line:90%
How about this one?

00:50:45.790 --> 00:50:49.330 align:middle line:84%
"A guard seems to be standing
in front of every building."

00:50:49.330 --> 00:50:50.320 align:middle line:90%
Is that ambiguous?

00:50:50.320 --> 00:51:01.970 align:middle line:90%


00:51:01.970 --> 00:51:03.940 align:middle line:90%
I think so.

00:51:03.940 --> 00:51:09.350 align:middle line:84%
So it means either "Looks like
every building is guarded"

00:51:09.350 --> 00:51:14.540 align:middle line:90%
or "Man, that guard is wide!"

00:51:14.540 --> 00:51:16.550 align:middle line:84%
or "Man, that guard
is running hard

00:51:16.550 --> 00:51:18.440 align:middle line:84%
to be in front of every
building at once!"

00:51:18.440 --> 00:51:21.110 align:middle line:84%
or "Look how clever that
guard is at geometry.

00:51:21.110 --> 00:51:23.930 align:middle line:84%
He's managed to stand in front
of every building at once!"

00:51:23.930 --> 00:51:28.050 align:middle line:84%
(Lucky thing for him,
they all face each other.)

00:51:28.050 --> 00:51:33.710 align:middle line:84%
So it has two meanings,
one with a very wide

00:51:33.710 --> 00:51:35.912 align:middle line:84%
or clever or fast-moving
guard, and then

00:51:35.912 --> 00:51:37.370 align:middle line:84%
the other more
normal reading where

00:51:37.370 --> 00:51:38.915 align:middle line:84%
it's like every
building has a guard.

00:51:38.915 --> 00:51:40.790 align:middle line:84%
So either every building
is such that there's

00:51:40.790 --> 00:51:42.290 align:middle line:84%
a guard standing in
front of it, or there

00:51:42.290 --> 00:51:43.707 align:middle line:84%
is a guard who is
somehow standing

00:51:43.707 --> 00:51:45.890 align:middle line:90%
in front of every building.

00:51:45.890 --> 00:51:47.360 align:middle line:90%
So yes, this is ambiguous.

00:51:47.360 --> 00:51:50.740 align:middle line:90%


00:51:50.740 --> 00:51:52.030 align:middle line:90%
How about this one?

00:51:52.030 --> 00:51:53.473 align:middle line:84%
"I seem to a guard
to be standing

00:51:53.473 --> 00:51:54.640 align:middle line:90%
in front of every building."

00:51:54.640 --> 00:51:57.640 align:middle line:90%


00:51:57.640 --> 00:52:00.010 align:middle line:90%
Is that ambiguous?

00:52:00.010 --> 00:52:01.510 align:middle line:90%
No.

00:52:01.510 --> 00:52:03.340 align:middle line:84%
This is a sentence
about one guard

00:52:03.340 --> 00:52:06.640 align:middle line:84%
who needs rest, who
needs to be taken

00:52:06.640 --> 00:52:10.270 align:middle line:84%
off-duty and convinced
to get some rest.

00:52:10.270 --> 00:52:12.460 align:middle line:84%
This is a guard who
thinks that, I don't know,

00:52:12.460 --> 00:52:14.320 align:middle line:84%
maybe he goes to one
building and I'm there

00:52:14.320 --> 00:52:16.778 align:middle line:84%
and he goes to another building
and I seem to be there too,

00:52:16.778 --> 00:52:19.810 align:middle line:84%
and he's seeing me everywhere
and this guard has problems.

00:52:19.810 --> 00:52:21.180 align:middle line:90%
And besides-- yes?

00:52:21.180 --> 00:52:23.140 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is it just
because "a guard" could

00:52:23.140 --> 00:52:26.410 align:middle line:84%
be referring to multiple
guards whereas I-- you can't

00:52:26.410 --> 00:52:29.765 align:middle line:90%
have more than one I?

00:52:29.765 --> 00:52:31.390 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: But
the question still

00:52:31.390 --> 00:52:33.430 align:middle line:90%
remains, the other reading--

00:52:33.430 --> 00:52:34.510 align:middle line:90%
you're absolutely right.

00:52:34.510 --> 00:52:36.052 align:middle line:84%
But the other reading
that this could

00:52:36.052 --> 00:52:39.520 align:middle line:84%
have would be one with
something like every building

00:52:39.520 --> 00:52:42.310 align:middle line:84%
has this property:
I seem to a guard

00:52:42.310 --> 00:52:44.467 align:middle line:90%
to be standing in front of it.

00:52:44.467 --> 00:52:46.050 align:middle line:84%
That would be a
reading that would be,

00:52:46.050 --> 00:52:48.303 align:middle line:84%
like, "For this
building, there's

00:52:48.303 --> 00:52:50.470 align:middle line:84%
a guard who thinks that I'm
standing in front of it.

00:52:50.470 --> 00:52:51.750 align:middle line:84%
And for this other
building, there's

00:52:51.750 --> 00:52:53.375 align:middle line:84%
another guard, maybe
a different guard,

00:52:53.375 --> 00:52:55.260 align:middle line:84%
who thinks that I'm
standing in front of it."

00:52:55.260 --> 00:52:58.740 align:middle line:84%
That's a story
where there are lots

00:52:58.740 --> 00:53:03.300 align:middle line:84%
of guards who don't
necessarily believe

00:53:03.300 --> 00:53:04.830 align:middle line:84%
that I am
simultaneously standing

00:53:04.830 --> 00:53:06.080 align:middle line:90%
in front of lots of buildings.

00:53:06.080 --> 00:53:08.007 align:middle line:84%
They just all--
their hallucinations

00:53:08.007 --> 00:53:09.090 align:middle line:90%
are more simple than that.

00:53:09.090 --> 00:53:11.007 align:middle line:84%
They just all see me in
front of one building,

00:53:11.007 --> 00:53:13.132 align:middle line:84%
one particular building,
maybe the building they're

00:53:13.132 --> 00:53:14.250 align:middle line:90%
supposed to be guarding.

00:53:14.250 --> 00:53:15.667 align:middle line:84%
All of them are
taking the day off

00:53:15.667 --> 00:53:17.970 align:middle line:84%
because they're like, oh
yeah, that Richards guy, he's

00:53:17.970 --> 00:53:20.300 align:middle line:90%
guarding that building.

00:53:20.300 --> 00:53:20.930 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:53:20.930 --> 00:53:23.303 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:53:23.303 --> 00:53:24.220 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah?

00:53:24.220 --> 00:53:26.020 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Somehow it
didn't register to me

00:53:26.020 --> 00:53:27.560 align:middle line:90%
that you were the one--

00:53:27.560 --> 00:53:30.173 align:middle line:90%
not the guard.

00:53:30.173 --> 00:53:32.840 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah, so that's
what the second sentence means.

00:53:32.840 --> 00:53:34.757 align:middle line:84%
But the point is that
it only means one thing.

00:53:34.757 --> 00:53:37.120 align:middle line:84%
It means there is one guard
who thinks that I'm standing

00:53:37.120 --> 00:53:38.245 align:middle line:90%
in front of every building.

00:53:38.245 --> 00:53:40.822 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't mean every
building is such

00:53:40.822 --> 00:53:42.280 align:middle line:84%
that there is some
guard who thinks

00:53:42.280 --> 00:53:43.700 align:middle line:90%
I'm standing in front of it.

00:53:43.700 --> 00:53:44.470 align:middle line:90%
Doesn't mean that.

00:53:44.470 --> 00:53:45.690 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:53:45.690 --> 00:53:51.420 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Could it be
that a guard [INAUDIBLE]??

00:53:51.420 --> 00:53:53.760 align:middle line:84%
It's a singular
phrase, but it could

00:53:53.760 --> 00:54:00.560 align:middle line:84%
refer to any number of people,
whereas "I seem to a guard"

00:54:00.560 --> 00:54:04.010 align:middle line:84%
means that there is one entity
that "a guard" refers to

00:54:04.010 --> 00:54:05.180 align:middle line:90%
and it can't be anyone else.

00:54:05.180 --> 00:54:09.253 align:middle line:84%
There is a person who has
this quality that saw you.

00:54:09.253 --> 00:54:10.670 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Yeah, so that's--

00:54:10.670 --> 00:54:13.190 align:middle line:84%
I think there was a
related comment earlier,

00:54:13.190 --> 00:54:14.810 align:middle line:84%
and I think that's
onto something,

00:54:14.810 --> 00:54:18.890 align:middle line:84%
but it's describing the
problem that we have.

00:54:18.890 --> 00:54:21.748 align:middle line:84%
If it's "A guard is standing
in front of every building,"

00:54:21.748 --> 00:54:24.290 align:middle line:84%
or "A guard seems to be standing
in front of every building,"

00:54:24.290 --> 00:54:27.020 align:middle line:84%
then yeah, "a guard" can
either be one guard that's

00:54:27.020 --> 00:54:29.720 align:middle line:84%
in many places at once
or it can be many guards,

00:54:29.720 --> 00:54:30.710 align:middle line:90%
one for each building.

00:54:30.710 --> 00:54:33.410 align:middle line:90%
That's the original ambiguity.

00:54:33.410 --> 00:54:36.080 align:middle line:84%
"A guard" has that power
that it can do that.

00:54:36.080 --> 00:54:38.690 align:middle line:84%
But why can't it do it
in the second sentence

00:54:38.690 --> 00:54:39.590 align:middle line:90%
is the question?

00:54:39.590 --> 00:54:43.970 align:middle line:84%
Why can't the
second sentence mean

00:54:43.970 --> 00:54:47.060 align:middle line:84%
there are three guards--
let's call them A, B, and C,

00:54:47.060 --> 00:54:49.520 align:middle line:84%
and guard A thinks that I'm
standing in front of building

00:54:49.520 --> 00:54:52.090 align:middle line:84%
number one, and guard B thinks
that I'm standing in front

00:54:52.090 --> 00:54:53.840 align:middle line:84%
of building number
two, and guard C thinks

00:54:53.840 --> 00:54:57.760 align:middle line:84%
that I'm standing in front
of building number three.

00:54:57.760 --> 00:55:00.670 align:middle line:84%
Why can't it mean that
when the first sentence can

00:55:00.670 --> 00:55:03.080 align:middle line:84%
mean A seems to be standing
in front of building one,

00:55:03.080 --> 00:55:05.080 align:middle line:84%
B seems to be standing
in front of building two,

00:55:05.080 --> 00:55:07.163 align:middle line:84%
C seems to be standing in
front of building three.

00:55:07.163 --> 00:55:09.640 align:middle line:84%
We can play that game, the
alphabet and number game,

00:55:09.640 --> 00:55:12.500 align:middle line:84%
with the first example, but
not with the second example.

00:55:12.500 --> 00:55:15.420 align:middle line:90%
We're trying to figure out why.

00:55:15.420 --> 00:55:19.030 align:middle line:84%
Yep, I'm trying
to figure out why.

00:55:19.030 --> 00:55:22.940 align:middle line:84%
Here's a plausible
story about why.

00:55:22.940 --> 00:55:25.970 align:middle line:84%
We went through this very
fast, so I don't blame anybody

00:55:25.970 --> 00:55:27.620 align:middle line:90%
if you don't remember this.

00:55:27.620 --> 00:55:29.848 align:middle line:84%
But when we were
doing "A guard seems

00:55:29.848 --> 00:55:31.640 align:middle line:84%
to be standing in front
of every building,"

00:55:31.640 --> 00:55:32.750 align:middle line:90%
sentences like that--

00:55:32.750 --> 00:55:40.770 align:middle line:90%


00:55:40.770 --> 00:55:45.390 align:middle line:84%
what we said was that there
is a movement operation going

00:55:45.390 --> 00:55:48.720 align:middle line:84%
on in sentences involving
"seems," this kind of sentence

00:55:48.720 --> 00:55:52.590 align:middle line:84%
involving "seems," where "seem"
is followed by an infinitive.

00:55:52.590 --> 00:55:57.060 align:middle line:84%
What's going on is that
"the guard," the thing that

00:55:57.060 --> 00:55:59.850 align:middle line:84%
ends up in subject
position, starts out

00:55:59.850 --> 00:56:00.990 align:middle line:90%
in the embedded clause.

00:56:00.990 --> 00:56:06.060 align:middle line:84%
Here is a guard, and it's
raising, moving up to here.

00:56:06.060 --> 00:56:09.570 align:middle line:84%
There's NP movement--
so a movement.

00:56:09.570 --> 00:56:12.810 align:middle line:84%
I'm sorry, I know it's
dangerous taking 24.900 from

00:56:12.810 --> 00:56:15.390 align:middle line:84%
a syntactician, because I keep
trying to change the subject

00:56:15.390 --> 00:56:16.877 align:middle line:90%
back to syntax.

00:56:16.877 --> 00:56:18.960 align:middle line:84%
So now we're going to talk
about syntax some more.

00:56:18.960 --> 00:56:20.835 align:middle line:84%
This is a bit of syntax
that we talked about.

00:56:20.835 --> 00:56:23.977 align:middle line:84%
I tried to convince you that
sentences like this, sentences

00:56:23.977 --> 00:56:25.560 align:middle line:84%
with "seem" and an
infinitive in them,

00:56:25.560 --> 00:56:27.870 align:middle line:84%
the subject of the
whole thing started out

00:56:27.870 --> 00:56:28.890 align:middle line:90%
in the embedded clause.

00:56:28.890 --> 00:56:30.270 align:middle line:84%
One of the kinds
of arguments had

00:56:30.270 --> 00:56:32.820 align:middle line:84%
to do with properties of idioms,
that you could say things

00:56:32.820 --> 00:56:41.350 align:middle line:84%
like "The shit seems
to have hit the fan,"

00:56:41.350 --> 00:56:44.080 align:middle line:84%
where we think that the
idiom is "The shit hit

00:56:44.080 --> 00:56:48.730 align:middle line:84%
the fan," that "the shit"
needs to be somewhere near

00:56:48.730 --> 00:56:51.293 align:middle line:84%
"hit the fan," but it's way
over there, and the story was,

00:56:51.293 --> 00:56:53.710 align:middle line:84%
yeah, that's because it starts
out in the embedded clause,

00:56:53.710 --> 00:56:57.700 align:middle line:84%
and it undergoes NP movement
into the matrix clause.

00:56:57.700 --> 00:56:59.560 align:middle line:90%
So it moves, it raises.

00:56:59.560 --> 00:57:01.000 align:middle line:90%
That sound familiar at all?

00:57:01.000 --> 00:57:05.297 align:middle line:84%
Have you all expunged the
terrible memory of syntax?

00:57:05.297 --> 00:57:06.880 align:middle line:84%
That's the way we
were talking before.

00:57:06.880 --> 00:57:09.420 align:middle line:90%


00:57:09.420 --> 00:57:14.480 align:middle line:84%
So we said a bit ago
that QR is clause-bound.

00:57:14.480 --> 00:57:15.080 align:middle line:90%
There we go.

00:57:15.080 --> 00:57:16.610 align:middle line:84%
That's us saying the
QR is clause-bound.

00:57:16.610 --> 00:57:17.690 align:middle line:84%
"The guard said
that I should stand

00:57:17.690 --> 00:57:18.620 align:middle line:90%
in front of every building."

00:57:18.620 --> 00:57:19.280 align:middle line:90%
I said yeah.

00:57:19.280 --> 00:57:20.690 align:middle line:90%
Why isn't that ambiguous?

00:57:20.690 --> 00:57:23.930 align:middle line:84%
Well, it's not ambiguous because
"every building" is inside

00:57:23.930 --> 00:57:26.480 align:middle line:84%
an embedded clause that
doesn't contain "a guard."

00:57:26.480 --> 00:57:29.255 align:middle line:90%


00:57:29.255 --> 00:57:31.850 align:middle line:84%
So QR can't take
"every building"

00:57:31.850 --> 00:57:34.790 align:middle line:90%
and move it past "a guard."

00:57:34.790 --> 00:57:37.950 align:middle line:84%
And then I think I also said, if
we're saying things like that,

00:57:37.950 --> 00:57:39.440 align:middle line:84%
we eventually will
have to explain

00:57:39.440 --> 00:57:42.500 align:middle line:84%
what kinds of things-- what
we mean by clauses, exactly.

00:57:42.500 --> 00:57:44.660 align:middle line:84%
So clause has to include
things like that--

00:57:44.660 --> 00:57:47.940 align:middle line:84%
I should stand in front
of every building.

00:57:47.940 --> 00:57:53.608 align:middle line:84%
What about a sentence
like "A guard

00:57:53.608 --> 00:57:55.650 align:middle line:84%
seems to be standing in
front of every building"?

00:57:55.650 --> 00:57:57.193 align:middle line:90%
Well, here we get ambiguity.

00:57:57.193 --> 00:57:59.610 align:middle line:84%
And it could be that we're
getting ambiguity here because,

00:57:59.610 --> 00:58:13.575 align:middle line:84%
well, "every building" has the
option of undergoing QR here.

00:58:13.575 --> 00:58:17.150 align:middle line:90%


00:58:17.150 --> 00:58:19.430 align:middle line:84%
The chalk I'm using
is very small.

00:58:19.430 --> 00:58:21.920 align:middle line:84%
I'm blaming my
illegible handwriting

00:58:21.920 --> 00:58:24.955 align:middle line:90%
on the poor quality of my chalk.

00:58:24.955 --> 00:58:27.080 align:middle line:84%
It's foolish of me to pick
up a new piece of chalk,

00:58:27.080 --> 00:58:28.400 align:middle line:84%
because that will
blow that excuse out

00:58:28.400 --> 00:58:29.650 align:middle line:90%
of the water in just a second.

00:58:29.650 --> 00:58:32.610 align:middle line:90%


00:58:32.610 --> 00:58:35.840 align:middle line:90%
So here's "a guard" in the end.

00:58:35.840 --> 00:58:37.370 align:middle line:84%
It could be that
there's ambiguity

00:58:37.370 --> 00:58:39.440 align:middle line:84%
in "a guard seems to be standing
in front of every building"

00:58:39.440 --> 00:58:41.270 align:middle line:84%
because "every building"
can undergo QR up

00:58:41.270 --> 00:58:45.570 align:middle line:84%
to here to a position
above "every guard."

00:58:45.570 --> 00:58:47.850 align:middle line:84%
If that's possible, then
that would be telling us

00:58:47.850 --> 00:58:50.910 align:middle line:84%
that well this TP down
here, "to be standing in

00:58:50.910 --> 00:58:53.490 align:middle line:84%
front of every building"
doesn't count as a clause

00:58:53.490 --> 00:58:55.420 align:middle line:90%
that you can QR out of that.

00:58:55.420 --> 00:58:57.120 align:middle line:84%
So when we say that
QR is clause-bound,

00:58:57.120 --> 00:58:59.010 align:middle line:84%
we don't mean this
kind of thing.

00:58:59.010 --> 00:59:01.460 align:middle line:90%
We mean tensed clauses.

00:59:01.460 --> 00:59:04.760 align:middle line:84%
But another possibility
is that we do actually

00:59:04.760 --> 00:59:08.180 align:middle line:84%
mean tensed clauses and that
the reason we do actually

00:59:08.180 --> 00:59:12.530 align:middle line:84%
need every TP, that QR out
of this TP is impossible,

00:59:12.530 --> 00:59:15.020 align:middle line:84%
and that it's not
possible to QR up to here,

00:59:15.020 --> 00:59:16.820 align:middle line:90%
it's only possible to QR here.

00:59:16.820 --> 00:59:26.870 align:middle line:90%


00:59:26.870 --> 00:59:28.970 align:middle line:84%
And that the reason
that you get ambiguity

00:59:28.970 --> 00:59:31.550 align:middle line:84%
in "A guard seems to be standing
in front of every building"

00:59:31.550 --> 00:59:32.540 align:middle line:90%
goes like this--

00:59:32.540 --> 00:59:34.790 align:middle line:84%
"every building"
can QR to a position

00:59:34.790 --> 00:59:37.070 align:middle line:84%
in the periphery of
that embedded clause,

00:59:37.070 --> 00:59:40.750 align:middle line:84%
and "a guard" started out
in that embedded clause.

00:59:40.750 --> 00:59:43.290 align:middle line:90%
And that's enough.

00:59:43.290 --> 00:59:46.545 align:middle line:84%
This would be a story
where we're already--

00:59:46.545 --> 00:59:49.897 align:middle line:84%
if we're doing QR at all,
we're slowly, hopefully,

00:59:49.897 --> 00:59:52.230 align:middle line:84%
getting accustomed to the
idea that part of interpreting

00:59:52.230 --> 00:59:55.350 align:middle line:84%
sentences will be interpreting
things in places where we

00:59:55.350 --> 00:59:58.090 align:middle line:90%
cannot necessarily see them.

00:59:58.090 --> 01:00:00.720 align:middle line:84%
So we can take things and move
them invisibly to other places

01:00:00.720 --> 01:00:02.950 align:middle line:90%
and interpret them there.

01:00:02.950 --> 01:00:04.500 align:middle line:84%
So in "A guard
seems to be standing

01:00:04.500 --> 01:00:05.940 align:middle line:84%
in front of every
building," there

01:00:05.940 --> 01:00:08.590 align:middle line:84%
are two invisible
movements going on.

01:00:08.590 --> 01:00:10.640 align:middle line:84%
One is we are invisibly
moving "every building,"

01:00:10.640 --> 01:00:12.390 align:middle line:84%
maybe not all the way
up to here, but just

01:00:12.390 --> 01:00:15.210 align:middle line:84%
to the edge of the embedded
clause, the embedded TP.

01:00:15.210 --> 01:00:19.020 align:middle line:84%
And we are invisibly taking
"a guard," which started out

01:00:19.020 --> 01:00:21.370 align:middle line:84%
in the embedded subject
position and moved up to here

01:00:21.370 --> 01:00:25.010 align:middle line:84%
and underwent NP movement,
and we're putting it back.

01:00:25.010 --> 01:00:27.135 align:middle line:84%
So we end up with "every
building" above "a guard."

01:00:27.135 --> 01:00:30.050 align:middle line:90%


01:00:30.050 --> 01:00:32.000 align:middle line:90%
And the reason to take that--

01:00:32.000 --> 01:00:32.750 align:middle line:90%
you're stunned.

01:00:32.750 --> 01:00:34.250 align:middle line:84%
I don't blame you
for being stunned.

01:00:34.250 --> 01:00:37.070 align:middle line:84%
So there are these two
invisible things happening.

01:00:37.070 --> 01:00:40.070 align:middle line:84%
A reason to take
that idea seriously

01:00:40.070 --> 01:00:42.140 align:middle line:84%
is the fact at the
bottom of this.

01:00:42.140 --> 01:00:43.850 align:middle line:84%
So "I seem to a
guard to be standing

01:00:43.850 --> 01:00:46.490 align:middle line:84%
in front of every building--"
we can get a story

01:00:46.490 --> 01:00:48.650 align:middle line:84%
about why we're not getting
ambiguity here if we're

01:00:48.650 --> 01:00:51.530 align:middle line:84%
willing to say no, QR
can't, in fact, get out

01:00:51.530 --> 01:00:53.783 align:middle line:84%
of an infinitival clause
like "to be standing

01:00:53.783 --> 01:00:54.950 align:middle line:90%
in front of every building."

01:00:54.950 --> 01:00:57.410 align:middle line:84%
It can only get to the
edge of such a clause.

01:00:57.410 --> 01:00:58.970 align:middle line:84%
And what's special
about this example

01:00:58.970 --> 01:01:02.717 align:middle line:84%
is that "a guard" is not
inside the embedded clause

01:01:02.717 --> 01:01:04.550 align:middle line:84%
"to be standing in front
of every building."

01:01:04.550 --> 01:01:07.520 align:middle line:90%
It's an argument of "seem."

01:01:07.520 --> 01:01:10.563 align:middle line:90%
So it's in the higher clause.

01:01:10.563 --> 01:01:12.480 align:middle line:84%
I don't know why I did
this with a blackboard.

01:01:12.480 --> 01:01:13.770 align:middle line:90%
I think I have slides here.

01:01:13.770 --> 01:01:14.710 align:middle line:90%
So let me show you the slides.

01:01:14.710 --> 01:01:15.585 align:middle line:90%
Maybe they will help.

01:01:15.585 --> 01:01:18.750 align:middle line:90%


01:01:18.750 --> 01:01:21.860 align:middle line:84%
So we think that "a
guard seems to be

01:01:21.860 --> 01:01:23.360 align:middle line:84%
standing in front
of every building"

01:01:23.360 --> 01:01:24.990 align:middle line:90%
involves a step of movement.

01:01:24.990 --> 01:01:27.480 align:middle line:84%
"A guard" is moving out
of the embedded clause

01:01:27.480 --> 01:01:29.415 align:middle line:84%
and into the matrix
subject position.

01:01:29.415 --> 01:01:32.390 align:middle line:90%


01:01:32.390 --> 01:01:35.175 align:middle line:84%
And I see now I don't
actually have slides

01:01:35.175 --> 01:01:36.300 align:middle line:90%
that help with any of this.

01:01:36.300 --> 01:01:38.990 align:middle line:84%
So the reason we're
getting this ambiguity

01:01:38.990 --> 01:01:41.840 align:middle line:84%
is not that "every building"
can QR past the higher

01:01:41.840 --> 01:01:42.980 align:middle line:90%
position of "a guard."

01:01:42.980 --> 01:01:45.500 align:middle line:84%
It can only QR past the
lower position of "a guard,"

01:01:45.500 --> 01:01:47.100 align:middle line:90%
and that's good enough.

01:01:47.100 --> 01:01:49.460 align:middle line:84%
So we get to interpret the
lower position of "a guard"

01:01:49.460 --> 01:01:53.150 align:middle line:84%
as well as the higher invisible
position of "every building."

01:01:53.150 --> 01:01:55.405 align:middle line:84%
So we get a handle
on all of these facts

01:01:55.405 --> 01:01:56.780 align:middle line:84%
as long as we're
willing to posit

01:01:56.780 --> 01:02:00.470 align:middle line:84%
these particular invisible
kinds of movements.

01:02:00.470 --> 01:02:02.990 align:middle line:84%
Learning something about QR--
when we say it's clause-bound,

01:02:02.990 --> 01:02:06.350 align:middle line:84%
we mean it can't get any
further than TP, any kind of TP,

01:02:06.350 --> 01:02:07.645 align:middle line:90%
tensed or nontensed.

01:02:07.645 --> 01:02:10.900 align:middle line:90%


01:02:10.900 --> 01:02:12.940 align:middle line:84%
Now Danny Fox also
discovered something else

01:02:12.940 --> 01:02:14.890 align:middle line:90%
that's cool about QR.

01:02:14.890 --> 01:02:17.300 align:middle line:84%
He's discovered
many cool things.

01:02:17.300 --> 01:02:20.570 align:middle line:84%
And the next thing is
quite long and complicated,

01:02:20.570 --> 01:02:23.980 align:middle line:84%
so maybe I will pause
here and take questions.

01:02:23.980 --> 01:02:25.480 align:middle line:84%
Do people have
questions about this?

01:02:25.480 --> 01:02:29.900 align:middle line:84%
Would you like me to go through
it more slowly or carefully?

01:02:29.900 --> 01:02:34.437 align:middle line:84%
I've been talking so far
as QR is always optional.

01:02:34.437 --> 01:02:36.520 align:middle line:84%
Danny Fox has discovered--
it was one of his early

01:02:36.520 --> 01:02:37.902 align:middle line:90%
discoveries--

01:02:37.902 --> 01:02:39.610 align:middle line:84%
it's actually more
complicated than that.

01:02:39.610 --> 01:02:41.860 align:middle line:84%
What we'll see, and we'll
start doing this next time,

01:02:41.860 --> 01:02:45.790 align:middle line:84%
is that you can
only do QR if it's

01:02:45.790 --> 01:02:49.160 align:middle line:84%
going to change the
meaning of the sentence.

01:02:49.160 --> 01:02:51.550 align:middle line:84%
So if you have two
qualifiers, you

01:02:51.550 --> 01:02:54.740 align:middle line:84%
can QR the lower quantifier
past the higher quantifier

01:02:54.740 --> 01:02:56.740 align:middle line:84%
if that's going to give
you a different meaning,

01:02:56.740 --> 01:03:00.910 align:middle line:84%
a meaning you wouldn't have
had if the QR hadn't happened.

01:03:00.910 --> 01:03:03.580 align:middle line:84%
If you have a sentence where
QR wouldn't affect the meaning,

01:03:03.580 --> 01:03:04.510 align:middle line:90%
it can't happen.

01:03:04.510 --> 01:03:08.000 align:middle line:90%


01:03:08.000 --> 01:03:09.560 align:middle line:84%
And what we'll do
next time-- we'll

01:03:09.560 --> 01:03:10.970 align:middle line:84%
start with this
next time-- we'll

01:03:10.970 --> 01:03:12.815 align:middle line:84%
develop the tools
to discover that,

01:03:12.815 --> 01:03:15.140 align:middle line:84%
the tools that Danny
Fox discovered,

01:03:15.140 --> 01:03:18.540 align:middle line:84%
and then I'll show
you that that's true.

01:03:18.540 --> 01:03:20.810 align:middle line:84%
So any further questions
about any of this?

01:03:20.810 --> 01:03:24.530 align:middle line:84%
At this point, you should
all be weirded out.

01:03:24.530 --> 01:03:26.780 align:middle line:84%
Suddenly things are moving
around in an invisible way,

01:03:26.780 --> 01:03:28.363 align:middle line:84%
and this is part of
interpreting them.

01:03:28.363 --> 01:03:29.840 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:03:29.840 --> 01:03:31.610 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: If QR
are can only happen

01:03:31.610 --> 01:03:39.330 align:middle line:84%
if it will change the meaning
of the sentence, that indicates

01:03:39.330 --> 01:03:42.450 align:middle line:84%
that the English language, and
I imagine other languages that

01:03:42.450 --> 01:03:49.440 align:middle line:84%
allow this, they build in
the ambiguity as opposed

01:03:49.440 --> 01:03:51.272 align:middle line:84%
to trying to mitigate
the ambiguity?

01:03:51.272 --> 01:03:52.980 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh,
I see what you mean.

01:03:52.980 --> 01:03:55.320 align:middle line:90%
I think I see what you mean.

01:03:55.320 --> 01:03:57.600 align:middle line:84%
I mean, the claim in the
end is that the structures

01:03:57.600 --> 01:04:01.020 align:middle line:84%
that are interpreted
are never ambiguous.

01:04:01.020 --> 01:04:03.870 align:middle line:90%
You either do QR or you don't.

01:04:03.870 --> 01:04:05.880 align:middle line:84%
And if you do QR, then
you get one reading.

01:04:05.880 --> 01:04:08.310 align:middle line:84%
And if you don't do QR, then
you get another reading.

01:04:08.310 --> 01:04:10.560 align:middle line:84%
And English is one of the
languages in which you don't

01:04:10.560 --> 01:04:13.800 align:middle line:84%
get to see QR, and the result is
that the sentence is ambiguous

01:04:13.800 --> 01:04:16.503 align:middle line:84%
because you can't tell whether
QR has happened or not.

01:04:16.503 --> 01:04:18.420 align:middle line:84%
There are languages out
there, like Hungarian,

01:04:18.420 --> 01:04:22.303 align:middle line:84%
where you can see QR, and so
the sentences are not ambiguous.

01:04:22.303 --> 01:04:24.720 align:middle line:84%
But yes, that means there are
certain kinds of ambiguities

01:04:24.720 --> 01:04:27.720 align:middle line:84%
in English that don't exist
in some other languages,

01:04:27.720 --> 01:04:28.560 align:middle line:90%
like Hungarian.

01:04:28.560 --> 01:04:29.610 align:middle line:84%
There are other
languages where it's

01:04:29.610 --> 01:04:30.735 align:middle line:90%
more complicated than that.

01:04:30.735 --> 01:04:35.760 align:middle line:84%
So there are languages
like Japanese,

01:04:35.760 --> 01:04:37.760 align:middle line:84%
and German, actually, has
a version of this too.

01:04:37.760 --> 01:04:41.840 align:middle line:84%
But in Japanese, if you
have two qualifiers,

01:04:41.840 --> 01:04:43.460 align:middle line:84%
their scope with
respect to each other

01:04:43.460 --> 01:04:47.270 align:middle line:84%
is unambiguous unless you move
one of them past the other,

01:04:47.270 --> 01:04:49.100 align:middle line:90%
and then it becomes ambiguous.

01:04:49.100 --> 01:04:53.120 align:middle line:84%
So basically, it's as though
the movement operation that

01:04:53.120 --> 01:04:56.270 align:middle line:84%
moves one quantifier past
the other could be QR

01:04:56.270 --> 01:04:58.170 align:middle line:90%
or it could be something else.

01:04:58.170 --> 01:05:01.130 align:middle line:84%
And so you can't tell whether
you have done QR of that thing

01:05:01.130 --> 01:05:01.740 align:middle line:90%
or not.

01:05:01.740 --> 01:05:04.400 align:middle line:84%
But there is no invisible
QR in Japanese--

01:05:04.400 --> 01:05:06.030 align:middle line:90%
something like that.

01:05:06.030 --> 01:05:07.610 align:middle line:90%
Cool.

01:05:07.610 --> 01:05:08.300 align:middle line:90%
All right, good.

01:05:08.300 --> 01:05:11.568 align:middle line:84%
Thank you for coming in braving
my possible cold viruses.

01:05:11.568 --> 01:05:13.110 align:middle line:84%
I apologize for
exposing you to them.

01:05:13.110 --> 01:05:16.900 align:middle line:90%
And I will see you on Thursday.

01:05:16.900 --> 01:05:24.000 align:middle line:90%